WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:08.000
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Welcome, welcome everybody, to&nbsp;
the public session of the NHS England and NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:17.680
improvement board. I’ve got apologies from Dido&nbsp;
and from Jeremy Townsend. Dido is taking a leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:25.120
of absence for six months from being chair of NHS&nbsp;
Improvement so she can focus her whole time on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.640
test and trace and on the National Institute&nbsp;
for Public Protection, and Andrew Morris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.033
who everybody knows, is stepping up to take her&nbsp;
place as acting chairman of NHS Improvement.&nbsp;

00:00:37.033 --> 00:01:15.360
[BREAK IN AUDIO] loss of connection audio resumes shortly

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.640
David Roberts, I wonder, do you want&nbsp;
to step in while David P reconnects?&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:27.520
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Fine, thank you. People can&nbsp;
hear me, I hope. So, yes. So Andrew Morris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:36.960
has taken over as, as the interim chair of NHS&nbsp;
Improvement. And, Andrew, thank you for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:44.160
Maybe if we could turn straightaway then&nbsp;
please, to the declarations of interest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.640
I don’t believe there are any that are not on the&nbsp;
register. But can I just confirm that. Thank you.&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:58.080
And then we have the minutes of the&nbsp;
meeting held on the first of October.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:09.280
Can we take those as a fair reflection? Yeah,&nbsp;
thank you very much. In which case, Simon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.680
over to you for your chief executive’s report.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Okay, thank you very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:22.960
David. So let me just start with an update on&nbsp;
the coronavirus situation. Over the last week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:29.840
we’ve seen Fortunately, the number of Covid&nbsp;
positive patients in hospital beginning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:36.240
stabilise, plateau at about 14500 number.&nbsp;
Of course, that compares with under 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.840
at the beginning of September.
And the geographical composition of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:49.760
inpatient count is changing. So we’re seeing&nbsp;
pleasing reductions in parts of the Northwest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:56.080
places like Liverpool, but we are seeing some&nbsp;
continuing increases in parts of the Midlands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.960
and particularly places such as East&nbsp;
Kent. So, that does all serve to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:11.360
underline the value of the local escalation&nbsp;
processes, which we are using to make almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.800
real-time judgments about the mix of health&nbsp;
services that can be provided in each area.&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:23.360
And as a consequence of that, we are&nbsp;
across the service as a whole able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:31.200
look after far more patients while managing nearly&nbsp;
15,000 Covid patients than was the case back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:39.360
March or April. So in fact, as of today,&nbsp;
we’ve got 20,000 more emergency inpatients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.720
other than Covid patients than we had at the&nbsp;
time when we last had 15,000 Covid inpatients.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:53.120
We’ve got three times more overnight elective&nbsp;
beds in use for routine operations. And we’ve got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:59.840
cancer treatments and GP appointments&nbsp;
mostly now back to usual levels.&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:07.440
But it does mean that we’ve got to ensure&nbsp;
that in the weeks leading up to Christmas, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:14.000
number of Covid inpatients overall continues&nbsp;
hopefully to come down in anticipation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.120
of further seasonal winter pressures that you’d&nbsp;
normally expect in January and February, and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:27.920
given the risks of a possible third Covid wave.
So the approach that we are taking, we will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.800
a chance to discuss further with Pauline and&nbsp;
Amanda later on the agenda. But I think it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.280
obviously right to say, as we usually do, a huge&nbsp;
thank you to all of our colleagues across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.240
health service who have been in many places&nbsp;
in the eye of a storm that has been greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.200
than the storm that they faced first time round&nbsp;
in the first wave of Covid. And of course this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:54.560
unfortunately has been a shared experience with&nbsp;
hospitals across Europe, which have also been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:02.080
filling up over the last two months. And that is&nbsp;
as true in France, Germany and the Netherlands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.880
and Belgium and Switzerland, as it is here in&nbsp;
England and the rest of the United Kingdom.&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:16.320
We have had positive signals from three vaccine&nbsp;
consortia over recent days, as you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:26.640
and our work to mobilise readiness for delivery&nbsp;
beginning on day one of availability is well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:34.720
advanced. The bulk of the vaccinations for at&nbsp;
risk groups, as the health secretary set out, will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:42.800
inevitably take place in January, March, April&nbsp;
timeframe based on the supply schedules that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:49.680
we’re expecting from the producers of the flu&nbsp;
vaccinations. But we do expect to be able to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:57.600
a strong start this side of Christmas subject to&nbsp;
the independent regulators’ licencing decisions.&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.360
And then I think the final thing I would say,&nbsp;
just by way of introduction is that, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:10.560
yesterday was the government’s spending review.&nbsp;
We have right from the start of this process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.200
of course, recognised that we already have a&nbsp;
multi-year revenue funding deal linked to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:26.960
long term plan. So we’re not looking to reopen&nbsp;
that in its own terms. But given the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:32.720
two things have obviously changed, one of&nbsp;
which being the extra costs of providing care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.880
linked to Covid itself, and we’ve had financial&nbsp;
support for that during the course of this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:45.360
and our agreement with government is that we will&nbsp;
continue to do so going into next year. And early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.560
in the new year, we’ll take stock of what those&nbsp;
costs are likely to be. So in the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:59.040
systems trusts should continue to plan on the&nbsp;
basis that they will sustain the capacity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.880
the staffing that they have available to them now.
And of course, the other thing that’s changed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:12.640
well as Covid costs and by the way, Covid costs&nbsp;
relating to PPE and to test and trace are funded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.880
direct by the department of health and social&nbsp;
care, given that those are not routed financially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.480
or operationally through NHS England and NHS&nbsp;
Improvement. But as well as the Covid costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:30.880
there are increasing health needs in&nbsp;
other areas, whether that’s the backlog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:38.800
for diagnostics and routine procedures, or&nbsp;
increasing evidence of the intensification of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.720
need for mental health services. And so we&nbsp;
were therefore, we welcome the fact that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.680
Chancellor has made available additional revenue&nbsp;
of billion and a half of additional revenue for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:57.360
those extra health needs going into next year.&nbsp;
We will be working, I’ll be working closely with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.440
Claire Murdoch and colleagues in the mental&nbsp;
health sector to identify the right priorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.400
for that additional half a billion of mental&nbsp;
health spending, which will be on top of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.920
already planned increases in mental health&nbsp;
services next year. And as part of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:17.120
we’ll be making the connection across to the&nbsp;
workforce growth that we need, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:22.400
sourced through Health Education England.
Two other final points on the spending review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:31.280
we were successful in securing £325 million of&nbsp;
additional capital investment for diagnostics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.200
which should enable us to make&nbsp;
a start on implementing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:40.080
Mike Richards diagnostic review that this board&nbsp;
discussed, I think at our last public meeting.&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:48.880
And in addition, pleased to see a continuation now&nbsp;
for the additional financial support for hospices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.960
which was so important to us in the first&nbsp;
half of this year during wave one of Covid.&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.800
So why don’t I stop there, see if there&nbsp;
are any points immediately arising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:05.600
otherwise a number of those themes will be&nbsp;
referred to during the rest of the discussion.&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.160
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Simon. And sorry for the&nbsp;
glitch on my technology at the beginning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:17.360
meeting. Simon, you went down to UCLH a couple&nbsp;
of weeks ago, to look at what they’re doing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:23.120
long Covid, treating patients with long Covid&nbsp;
symptoms, anything you can say about how we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:30.720
going to look after those patients going forwards?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes, thank you, David. Well, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:38.480
is, I think now beyond doubt that this condition&nbsp;
called long Covid does exist and does affect a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:46.160
small but significant minority of people who&nbsp;
have had Covid. Not necessarily those who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:51.120
hospitalised, let alone those who actually&nbsp;
had a positive Covid test early on in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:57.840
pandemic. And so with NICE we have&nbsp;
worked to establish a case definition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:04.320
to properly define what this group of patients and&nbsp;
condition looks like. And now the care pathways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:09.680
that are likely to be appropriate.
We’ve allocated an additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:13.360
£10 million pounds for the balance of the&nbsp;
year to support the creation of some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:20.000
long Covid clinics that are coming together in&nbsp;
different parts of the country. And Ian Dodge is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.760
actually going to be overseeing the rollout&nbsp;
of these long Covid services, which will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:31.840
in some cases, be driven off what’s happening in&nbsp;
primary and community services with the interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.840
with specialist care. In others, it is groups of&nbsp;
specialists in outpatients who’ve essentially come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:45.840
together in their area to create these services.&nbsp;
I don’t know if Ian wants to add to that.&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:52.240
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: The first of those clinics have&nbsp;
already gone live, Simon, and we are expecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:11:03.200
an initial and comprehensive network to roll&nbsp;
out during December and the New Year along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:10.800
the go live of the Your Recovery website,&nbsp;
particularly focused on pulmonary rehabilitation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:19.360
And there will be a lot of further work including&nbsp;
on research and the embedding of the pathways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.520
within primary and community as well&nbsp;
as specialist services. And I look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:29.520
forward to coming back to the board to update&nbsp;
colleagues on further progress in the new year.&nbsp;

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:35.680
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ian. Any other questions&nbsp;
for Simon, or we should move over to Amanda on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:41.600
operations. Amanda and Pauline?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:47.760
Thank you, David. And thank you, Simon&nbsp;
for your very comprehensive introduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:52.640
because I think that is really important that&nbsp;
in introducing this paper, which is about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:58.800
kind of operational performance which covers&nbsp;
Covid, winter, elective, it is absolutely in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:05.520
the context that Simon’s just set out about the&nbsp;
fact that we have had now for some time, a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.560
very significant number of patients with Covid in&nbsp;
our hospitals, as he said, around sort of 14,500.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:18.240
And I think he’s right to highlight that&nbsp;
actually the way that the NHS has worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:24.160
in partnership as through the first wave, so&nbsp;
that is absolutely at a local level, pulling in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:30.880
voluntary sector partners, patients themselves,&nbsp;
working very closely with local government to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:36.640
provide mutual aid to ensure that services&nbsp;
can be maintained wherever possible to support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:43.040
staff, has really made a difference through this&nbsp;
second wave, absolutely as it did in the first.&nbsp;

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:49.760
And so lots of things to talk about in that. But&nbsp;
the things that if I may I would just like to draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:56.800
attention to I think are, firstly, the importance&nbsp;
of the support for our staff through this not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:02.640
second wave but as we look forward for the&nbsp;
next few months, been a huge amount of work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:09.520
nationally, but also a huge amount of work locally&nbsp;
to put together packages of support for people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:14.320
are absolutely as Simon says in the eye of the&nbsp;
storm and continue to be and have relentlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:20.320
been now for some considerable period of time.
So the importance of putting care for our staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:24.000
at the heart of our response alongside&nbsp;
of course care for our patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:30.080
I think, has never been more important.
And secondly, I think it’s just worth saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:39.440
exactly as Simon’s called out, we have maintained&nbsp;
through this second wave the level of non-Covid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:45.280
care, particularly I’m thinking here elective&nbsp;
care, that we had managed to collectively achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:52.320
as we headed in to the second wave. So rather than&nbsp;
that significant reduction in activity that we saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:59.120
in the first wave, we’ve absolutely been able&nbsp;
to maintain that focus on, not on elective care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:06.320
as well. So that is, I think, a huge testament&nbsp;
actually to the commitment of colleagues across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:14.800
the NHS who have, who have made it possible and&nbsp;
made it their business to work together to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:20.880
and make sure that everyone who needed to be&nbsp;
seen could be seen over the last few months.&nbsp;

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:24.320
So what does that mean, in practice?&nbsp;
It means, and the paper calls this out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:30.560
it means that we have managed to improve and&nbsp;
increase use of the independent sector. It means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:34.480
that if we’re thinking about things that are&nbsp;
of a particular concern, I know to the board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:38.720
so things like cancer care, it&nbsp;
means that treatment levels both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:44.000
two-week wait and treatment levels back to pre&nbsp;
Covid levels. It means a huge amount of work done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:49.040
now around clinical validation. It’s been done&nbsp;
on the waiting list, moving on to diagnostics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:52.080
and that’s really about making&nbsp;
sure that individual patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:57.840
have confirmed their preference for treatment and&nbsp;
that there is improved communication then between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:03.760
the caregiver, the patient, their GP, their&nbsp;
carer, about what the plan is and what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:07.760
going to happen to them. So something that we’ve&nbsp;
heard very clearly fed back to us from patient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:12.640
groups that was important to them, as well as&nbsp;
being important to us to run services well.&nbsp;

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:21.040
It also means, again, real credit to Pauline and&nbsp;
her team on this, a hugely rapid rollout thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:26.640
to the availability of tests from Test and Trace&nbsp;
now of lateral flow testing across the NHS so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:31.840
asymptomatic staff can be tested regularly.&nbsp;
I’ll let Pauline talk more about that. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:37.760
at 25 million tests that have gone out over&nbsp;
the last, what, 10 days or so, to me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:41.440
we’re in a very different position in terms of&nbsp;
protecting our staff, protecting our patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:47.040
then that then, well, we have that&nbsp;
opportunity now that we didn’t before.&nbsp;

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:55.680
Other things it means. It means that on urgent&nbsp;
emergency care we are able, I think now and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.680 --> 00:16:00.880
Pauline would talk more about this, to be quite&nbsp;
confident about the rollout of 111 First, beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:07.280
the 16 sites that have already implemented that.&nbsp;
So December, that’s going to be a major focus.&nbsp;

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:13.360
Clearly, we’ve also got a major programme&nbsp;
of capital development in place in A&amp;E.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:20.320
However, I think it is worth saying, and the&nbsp;
board will be cited on this already I know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:26.640
that as you would expect at this time of year,&nbsp;
there is more pressure on the urgent emergency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:33.920
care pathway. And we are particularly concerned&nbsp;
about avoiding crowding in A&amp;E departments. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:40.480
would be anyway, but when you add the Covid risk&nbsp;
into the picture becomes particularly important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:46.320
for us to find those alternative ways to fight&nbsp;
to provide care for our patients. So 111 being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:50.880
a particularly good example of that, so that&nbsp;
people can get the care they need but without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:57.280
needing to present at the front door. And also&nbsp;
of course, we’re then managing flow through our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:01.360
hospitals and therefore the important role,&nbsp;
of course, our community colleagues play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:05.840
social care, in helping patients safely&nbsp;
discharge from hospital so that we’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:09.280
seeing that pressure build up at the front door&nbsp;
again. Pauline will want to say more about that.&nbsp;

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:14.880
Final couple of things to mention. And I think&nbsp;
it probably should have started rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:21.280
ended here. But we all know what an important role&nbsp;
primary care has played, GPs and their colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:29.440
over again this whole year. And now activity&nbsp;
in primary care back again to pre Covid levels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.160
And we should I think also just recognise the&nbsp;
particularly important role, not just in things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:39.920
like long Covid that Ian’s already mentioned and&nbsp;
the rollout of the Covid home oximetry services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:44.480
that’s happening now, but also the important role&nbsp;
they play in supporting the clinical extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:50.640
vulnerable patients. And that’s a group who,&nbsp;
again, primary care have a particularly important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:58.400
role, and particularly, we’ve relied on them&nbsp;
heavily this year to continue to do that.&nbsp;

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:05.600
And final point, Simon’s just made it but we are,&nbsp;
and again, I know the board’s very alert to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:11.120
but we have seen, are seeing significant increase&nbsp;
in demand for mental health services. And that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:17.600
again, particularly in the acute presentation.&nbsp;
So the very welcome additional funding for mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:23.680
health will certainly make a difference as we&nbsp;
look to the future. But right now, a big focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:28.080
not just on delivering those long term plan&nbsp;
commitments, but also in orientating the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:34.880
of the mental health team around things like the&nbsp;
crisis help lines for digital access to care, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:39.760
addition to the face to face routes to make sure&nbsp;
that the Covid specific needs are being addressed.&nbsp;

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:44.160
So I know that’s a quick run through but&nbsp;
I hope that’s okay in terms of picking up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:49.120
Covid a bit of where we are an urgent emergency&nbsp;
care, some of the risks, but also where we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:55.840
on the elective side of our responsibilities.&nbsp;
But let me hand over to Pauline, to pick up.&nbsp;

00:18:55.840 --> 00:19:01.680
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Thank you, Amanda. I don’t&nbsp;
want to repeat anything that Amanda or Simon have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:09.120
already covered. So I think just for me to start&nbsp;
off by acknowledging the amazing work that our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:16.960
staff are doing at all areas of care. I think wave&nbsp;
two has been significantly different to wave one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:21.600
insofar as people have had to deal with three&nbsp;
challenges, our staff have had to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:30.480
three challenges. Caring for our Covid patients as&nbsp;
Amanda described, but also during wave two there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:36.000
has been a major focus on being able to continue&nbsp;
our elective work. And clearly because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:42.880
timing of wave two, we are in the winter season&nbsp;
and seeing pressures in our emergency services.&nbsp;

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:46.800
So if you just look at what’s&nbsp;
happening in our emergency departments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:54.960
activity, attendances are up 68% on what we were&nbsp;
seeing last April, and admissions are up 42%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.840 --> 00:20:01.280
So I think that gives you a sense of how&nbsp;
different it is at this point of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:06.960
But, performance continues to be&nbsp;
better than it was this time last year.&nbsp;

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:17.440
Amanda made some references to 111 and the&nbsp;
rollout of 111 First during the month of December,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:24.080
and we have now been able to work with all of&nbsp;
our 111 providers, all of our systems throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:32.080
the country, to reach assurance that they have&nbsp;
the services in place to support that rollout.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:40.000
And at the moment, our core 111 services are&nbsp;
actually dealing with an 18.8% increase in calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:47.200
and doing that very well. And they’re managing&nbsp;
to maintain very good call answering standards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:52.160
and also really good standards with&nbsp;
regards to clinical input into calls.&nbsp;

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:58.960
Just staying on the theme of emergency care for&nbsp;
a minute, our ambulance services whilst seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:05.920
increases in demand, through the work that they’re&nbsp;
doing on see and treat and hear and treat pilots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:13.360
they’re able to reduce the number of conveyances&nbsp;
to our emergency departments. So basically able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:19.040
to find other pathways for patients to avoid&nbsp;
them needing to be brought into departments.&nbsp;

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:27.040
Moving on to maybe the last area that I wanted to&nbsp;
mention for UEC, and that’s our urgent treatment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:35.840
centres. And they will play a really significant&nbsp;
role as we roll out 111 First. We have now got 178&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:41.920
UTCs designated and 36 more&nbsp;
to go by the end of this year.&nbsp;

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:50.080
Just turning back to elective care, with&nbsp;
regards to the work that’s been done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:56.480
we’ve been able to maintain a waiting&nbsp;
list that’s smaller than the waiting list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.480 --> 00:22:01.920
we had this time last year. Now clearly&nbsp;
some of that is down to reduced demand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:09.520
But I think it’s also down to increased activity,&nbsp;
and people being able to bring work back on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:16.480
they weren’t able to do earlier in the year.&nbsp;
And using a number of different ways to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:22.400
coming up with very different ways of providing&nbsp;
care through the adapt and adopt work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:29.440
establishing things like an elective care hub&nbsp;
hubs, having a real focus on diagnostic work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:36.320
at the moment, we are doing 88% of last year’s&nbsp;
activity, which I think is pretty phenomenal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:44.960
And, you know, looking at different technologies,&nbsp;
different pathways, using the capital, the 150&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:52.720
million that was put into the system for that.
And one other thing just to mention is IS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:59.120
Amanda mentioned the fact that we are having&nbsp;
an increasing use of the independent sector&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:05.520
and working on a daily basis with our colleagues&nbsp;
to ensure that patients get seen in appropriate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:11.280
settings in the appropriate time spans.
And finally, to finish where Amanda started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:20.400
on supporting our staff. Yes, we are rolling out&nbsp;
testing, lateral flow testing. All of our staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:28.240
will have the opportunity to administer their own&nbsp;
test twice a week. If the test says that they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:35.600
positive, they will then go for PCR testing. We’ve&nbsp;
been able to roll out 25 million of these test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:43.680
kits to date to our acute services and mental&nbsp;
health services, our core community services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:51.120
and we hope to be able to continue into primary&nbsp;
care community, interested companies and indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:59.120
some visitors later this week and into next week.
And then finally, I think the other area where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:07.280
we’re very keen to support our stuff in advance of&nbsp;
any Covid vaccine is with the winter flu vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:14.160
and we’re working very hard to get the best&nbsp;
possible coverage across our services. Thank you.&nbsp;

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:19.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much for&nbsp;
that Amanda and Pauline. Ian Dodge.&nbsp;

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:28.800
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you. I just wanted to come in&nbsp;
briefly to amplify some of Amanda’s comments on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:35.600
the role of primary care. It’s seen an immense&nbsp;
achievement to have recovered back to pre Covid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:44.480
levels. We have by far the most successful winter&nbsp;
flu programme, led primarily by general practice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:52.240
We are successfully hiring lots of additional&nbsp;
staff under the additional role scheme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:59.440
General practice has also shown a really&nbsp;
strong response to the Covid vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.440 --> 00:25:06.800
programme. And we’ve put in £150 million&nbsp;
actually to further expand capacity. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:13.840
I think really worth recognising the immensity&nbsp;
of that contribution and our appreciation.&nbsp;

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:21.040
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ian. Now, Wol.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thank you, David. Amanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:25.680
thanks for your very fulsome report. I wonder&nbsp;
if it was worth just expanding a little bit on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:31.280
the building works and the connection with&nbsp;
infection prevention and control, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:36.240
making sure that the patient journey in&nbsp;
that part of our activities is very safe.&nbsp;

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:42.000
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you, Wol. I’ll let&nbsp;
Pauline perhaps just talk about a bit more of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:49.040
detail of what’s going on. But I think your kind&nbsp;
of point, headline point is a really important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:55.520
one, because of course, what the NHS has done over&nbsp;
the course of the past year is really reorganise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.520 --> 00:26:03.200
how it works into different pathways to keep&nbsp;
those kind of Covid secure pathways separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:08.640
from those pathways where patients are either&nbsp;
diagnosed as Covid positive, or there’s a risk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:13.440
And what that has meant is substantial changes to&nbsp;
the way that the estate is used, as well as how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:19.440
staff are deployed. So a really, really kind of&nbsp;
significant programme of work as a consequence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:25.840
Covid coming whenever it was, February time, March&nbsp;
time of last year, and what we’re now seeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:32.080
what we’re able to do with the capital investment&nbsp;
is begin to make the additional changes so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:35.680
actually, we’re in a more secure environment,&nbsp;
more resilient to going forward. But Pauline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:40.080
perhaps you could just pick up the detail.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes, just to maybe mention three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:48.240
areas. As far as an emergency care is concerned,&nbsp;
the first two, we’ve got 25 major schemes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.880 --> 00:26:54.240
in emergency departments. And so that’s really&nbsp;
schemes that are allowing those departments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.960 --> 00:27:01.120
to redesign, to reprofile their departments&nbsp;
for this winter to make sure that they’re safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:06.640
while we still have Covid in the system,&nbsp;
but also to make significant changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.640 --> 00:27:12.640
for the future delivery of emergency care.
At the same time, the second scheme, which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:19.840
sorry, the first scheme, first scheme, set of&nbsp;
schemes comes to 150 million. The second group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:27.520
projects comes to 300 million. And that basically&nbsp;
covers just about every emergency department that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.520 --> 00:27:35.440
we have. And again, it’s allowing them to focus on&nbsp;
how they have needed to redesign their department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:42.160
to make them safe during the Covid period,&nbsp;
but also to make changes that are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:47.520
be really valuable from an infection control&nbsp;
point of view and from a flow point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:52.400
moving forward post Covid.
I think just the third area to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:58.480
which I referred to earlier, is that it may not&nbsp;
be capital schemes per se, but we have invested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:06.560
150 million in diagnostic equipment, and in some&nbsp;
changes to facilitate the diagnostic equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.560 --> 00:28:12.480
And that was coming out of wave one of&nbsp;
Covid, realising and appreciating the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:17.920
that we were going to need to do to catch&nbsp;
up on some of the work that we hadn’t been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:22.880
able to do to the numbers that we would&nbsp;
have liked in the earlier part of the year.&nbsp;

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:31.680
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Pauline. David Roberts.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So Amanda, Pauline, thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:40.880
I’m interested in the where we’ve got to with NHS&nbsp;
111 First. And in particular, I’d be interested to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:49.200
hear how, what has been the reaction, what’s been&nbsp;
the changes in and improvements in operation as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:53.520
consequence of bringing it in? What’s been&nbsp;
the reaction of clinicians and critically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:59.040
what’s been the reaction of patients?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Should I answer that, Amanda?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:09.600
Okay, so basically 111 First, basically what 111&nbsp;
First is aiming to do is to encourage people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:15.920
that when they feel they have an urgent&nbsp;
or emergency care need to go online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:24.000
or actually call 111 to get advice.
By being able to do that, then we can support the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:32.000
caller to get the best possible pathway. And that&nbsp;
might mean asking somebody to go to a pharmacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:36.960
it might mean asking somebody to or linking&nbsp;
somebody up with a pathway of care that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:43.440
they’re already receiving, or it might mean that&nbsp;
somebody needs to go to an emergency department.&nbsp;

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:51.360
The significant difference under 111 First is&nbsp;
that for people who do need care in an emergency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:58.640
department, we will be able to book them in and&nbsp;
clearly it will depend on the availability of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:04.880
having sufficient booking slots, etc, which&nbsp;
we absolutely have been working on to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.880 --> 00:30:12.000
that that will be there and that we have the&nbsp;
staffing available to support this type of model.&nbsp;

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:19.040
So the work that we’ve done in the last&nbsp;
six months, last six to nine months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:26.560
is to prepare not only the 111 services for the&nbsp;
development of it, but all of the pathways of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:34.240
care, all of the directories of services that will&nbsp;
support the model. And as I referred to earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:41.520
David, we’ve gone through a fairly systematic&nbsp;
exercise in making sure that the staff exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:50.560
that call pathways have been redesigned, etc, the&nbsp;
directory of services is able to support it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:56.320
we roll it out in different parts of the country.
As Amanda referred to earlier, we’ve had a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.320 --> 00:31:02.720
of what we call first mover sites. And we have&nbsp;
learned from those as to what’s necessary, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.720 --> 00:31:11.520
ensure that we meet the needs of our patients and&nbsp;
the response of our clinicians. So we’ve listened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:17.760
to feedback from patients. We’ve listened to&nbsp;
feedback from our clinicians. And we have designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:23.520
as the days and weeks have gone by. I think&nbsp;
it’s fair to say that working with those 18&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:30.000
first movers, they have found this to be a very&nbsp;
good experience. But I think they’re all pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:37.520
us at this stage to go a little step further and&nbsp;
to be able to put more comms into the system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:43.840
Because I think local comms in this day and age&nbsp;
achieve so much, but national comms obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:50.960
is able to get our message across in a slightly&nbsp;
stronger way. So what we will do early in December&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:56.160
is to start rolling that out in a measured way.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: And given how sensitive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.160 --> 00:31:59.840
Pauline, what’s the average call&nbsp;
wait time for NHS 111 First?&nbsp;

00:32:00.640 --> 00:32:08.480
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: I have written down in front&nbsp;
of me, and I’m going to have to come back to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:11.200
But I definitely have that piece of information.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Don’t worry. Don’t worry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:15.120
Come back to me later.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: But can I say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:21.840
we have returned to our call answering times from&nbsp;
earlier in the year. Clearly we had some problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.840 --> 00:32:27.520
at the very start of the Covid period,&nbsp;
but we’re back to our really good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.520 --> 00:32:31.760
performance. I’ll get you the number in a second.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: If it comes to you later on, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:38.720
fill us in, Pauline. Ara, you’ve got a question.
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Thank you chair. Well, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:46.640
firstly, I’d like to, me as a clinician working&nbsp;
here, at St Mary’s, just to thank Amanda and Simon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.640 --> 00:32:52.400
and the whole team for really transforming&nbsp;
my life from wave one where I found myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:58.000
unemployed into actually starting to operate&nbsp;
with all the guidance and support they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:03.040
given us at a local level. So thank you for that.
On the other hand, I wanted to ask a question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.040 --> 00:33:08.960
what are our plans in many, many good&nbsp;
learning lessons that we’ve had during Covid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:17.360
that we can maintain as we see ourselves&nbsp;
through this dreadful disease post Covid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:24.080
which we have to retain because I think we’ve seen&nbsp;
the best in our NHS staff during Covid. We have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:24.080 --> 00:33:31.040
retain that somehow. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:33.680
Amanda, I think that’s probably for you.
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:38.960
I’m happy to start, but I suspect this might be&nbsp;
something that certainly Steve and Ruth might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:42.720
want to come in on as well, because I think,&nbsp;
and thank you for what you’ve just said, Ara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:47.840
but I think actually we should probably say&nbsp;
thank you for all that you do, and of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:51.840
as colleagues have said for all of our clinical&nbsp;
colleagues across the country, because I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:57.200
our job is entirely to try and make life as&nbsp;
easy as possible for people to do their jobs.&nbsp;

00:33:58.160 --> 00:34:03.440
So what, what do we need to hold on to?&nbsp;
Actually, I think I’d probably start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:07.760
clinical leadership. So my sense through the&nbsp;
first wave, and I think it’s been true through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.760 --> 00:34:13.040
the second as well is that what we have really&nbsp;
seen is multidisciplinary clinical leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:19.040
really helping to identify, whether that’s through&nbsp;
critical care networks or it’s down to that local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:24.240
place, working out what their population needs&nbsp;
and organising services around that. That’s one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.240 --> 00:34:29.200
of the things we must not lose sight of as a kind&nbsp;
of clear benefit that we need to build on and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:29.200 --> 00:34:34.160
go forward with and when we talk about the ICSs&nbsp;
later, hopefully some of that will come through.&nbsp;

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:38.320
I think clearly there’s a huge opportunity for&nbsp;
us to build on what’s happened through the use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:48.800
of technology in the last few months. I think&nbsp;
we’ve seen the importance of introducing digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:55.040
alongside and in the right way so that for&nbsp;
patients it becomes something that’s really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.040 --> 00:35:01.440
empowering and it’s not about finding ways to&nbsp;
force people into new ways of working if they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:06.640
not comfortable with it, but it is absolutely&nbsp;
about offering people different and in many cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:11.360
better ways of accessing services that are more&nbsp;
convenient that are around actually what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.360 --> 00:35:17.440
need from us rather than that more traditional&nbsp;
face to face model, which isn’t always the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:20.960
way. And I think we’ve really shown that&nbsp;
we need to build on that as we go forward.&nbsp;

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:24.000
I think there’s also something&nbsp;
for me about and, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:31.040
London is certainly at the vanguard of this,&nbsp;
really looking at how we organise services most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:31.040 --> 00:35:38.000
efficiently. So that is, when I say efficiently,&nbsp;
I do mean, in a sense that it’s both about value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:42.400
value for money, but also about quality&nbsp;
outcomes. So what we’ve seen, I think, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:47.360
way that London has responded, a clinically led&nbsp;
response to the pandemic, has been about trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:56.800
say, how do we get now some real momentum behind&nbsp;
high quality, consistent pathways of care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.800 --> 00:36:02.320
which do mean in some cases organising&nbsp;
services so that higher volumes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:06.960
activity are going through certain sites,&nbsp;
and that’s driving both better outcomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.960 --> 00:36:13.120
better patient experience, and also better value?
So I think those are the sorts of ideas that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:17.120
certainly London has been at the vanguard at, but&nbsp;
there are examples of that across the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:23.360
where, you know, I know that colleagues are&nbsp;
equally keen, to kind of build on the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:28.560
that they’ve done so far, and keep going with it.
The final one I’d call out, but I will let Steve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:34.560
and Ruth come in is I do think for me, it is this&nbsp;
final point about how do we really get serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:39.440
about the support for the health and wellbeing&nbsp;
of our staff. It’s been so much emphasis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.440 --> 00:36:45.120
and rightly so on that over the past year, but&nbsp;
it’s knitted absolutely, fundamentally with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:53.680
equality, diversity and inclusion agenda. So&nbsp;
making a serious leadership commitment to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:59.120
not just being a pandemic response, but being&nbsp;
about, as the people plan sets out, a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:03.520
we are committed to working for the long term,&nbsp;
I think would be the other area that feels to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:10.640
to be just a very obvious one to call out at this&nbsp;
point. But that’s top of mind stuff. I’m sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:14.160
Steve and Ruth will have other things to add.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Steve, do you want to – because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:18.720
Ruth is going to answer another question anyway,&nbsp;
I think, but Steve, have you got any reflections?&nbsp;

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:23.520
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Well, to be honest, I think&nbsp;
Amanda’s probably given almost all the examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:30.560
I would have given, so she’s sort of done the job&nbsp;
for me. And I agree completely with her analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:38.560
And there are lots of other examples at a sort&nbsp;
of more nuanced level than the system wide level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:38.560 --> 00:37:42.880
examples perhaps Amanda has given.
The only other comment I’d add is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:48.000
which is related is, is when we look back&nbsp;
to the long term plan, our 10 year strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.880 --> 00:37:56.880
for me, the pandemic has really highlighted the&nbsp;
need to just continue with the key priorities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.880 --> 00:38:00.880
the long term plan. I think almost all&nbsp;
the things that we wanted to focus on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:07.840
Amanda’s mentioned digital transformation as&nbsp;
one, but the equalities agenda, the prevention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:13.520
agenda are all things that I think Covid has&nbsp;
brought sharply into focus. And I personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:18.800
and I’m sure as a board, it’s just given us more&nbsp;
determination to go harder and faster with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:24.640
strategy as much as we possibly can.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Steve. Pauline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.640 --> 00:38:28.800
have you just found the answer to David&nbsp;
Roberts’ question, I think, have you?&nbsp;

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:34.400
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes. I knew I had it written&nbsp;
in front of me this morning. 79.1% of our calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:40.720
are answered within 60 seconds, David. So that&nbsp;
really takes us back to the really excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:46.400
performance we had earlier in the year.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Pauline. So Ruth.&nbsp;

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:53.920
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much. I’ll go back&nbsp;
into what Amanda and Steve and Ara was talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:53.920 --> 00:39:00.160
about. So for me, there’s been several bits of&nbsp;
learning, which has been an amazing flexibility of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.160 --> 00:39:05.920
our staff, and their willingness to do additional&nbsp;
training, whether that’s those people stepping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.920 --> 00:39:11.520
into critical care units, whether those people&nbsp;
stepping into work with our homeless people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:16.800
or into the community. People have gone and been&nbsp;
very, very flexible, come out to their normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:23.360
area of expertise and really have taken on&nbsp;
extra training, taken on extra responsibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.360 --> 00:39:27.840
and that has been something of a great source&nbsp;
of pride to me about how people have done that.&nbsp;

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:34.960
The big one as well for me is about a lesson&nbsp;
learned is around how local implementation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.960 --> 00:39:41.040
plans are so much more important. We can say&nbsp;
things nationally, we can say and we can plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:46.560
and policy drivers. But actually, unless&nbsp;
it’s backed by local implementation plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:51.840
we don’t get the same success as we could&nbsp;
have. So that’s a big lesson for me.&nbsp;

00:39:51.840 --> 00:39:56.000
[Inaudible] and infection prevention&nbsp;
control, those places have got more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:04.640
single rooms, have greater achievement of&nbsp;
maintaining excellence in infection control.&nbsp;

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:10.480
And of course, I’ve been so impressed by the&nbsp;
way over 20,000 student nurses and midwives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:19.120
joined the front line in wave one. And how&nbsp;
people did go over and above, retained their –&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:26.560
delayed their retirement, worked extra hours. And&nbsp;
I think our big challenge going forward is how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:32.640
not just continue to recruit people like that, but&nbsp;
how we retain them. And that’s why later on today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:36.720
we’re discussing health and wellbeing,&nbsp;
which is going to be an important plank.&nbsp;

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:42.800
My only other point, David, which is why my hand&nbsp;
was originally up was to go back to Amanda and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:47.680
Pauline’s conversation about just how much&nbsp;
work is going through the NHS right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:56.400
not just in patients with Covid, but&nbsp;
also, of course, those patients who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:56.400 --> 00:41:00.400
come in through the urgent care pathway&nbsp;
like they didn’t in so many ways before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:06.560
and indeed, doing elective care. And I’ve been&nbsp;
to some places recently that are very, very proud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.560 --> 00:41:10.400
of what they’re doing on their elective care&nbsp;
pathways, as well as their emergency pathways.&nbsp;

00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:16.560
And I just want to say a big thank you to all of&nbsp;
our staff. Our clinicians get a thank you lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.560 --> 00:41:21.600
of the times, but I just want to call out all of&nbsp;
our staff, whether they’re our portering staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.600 --> 00:41:26.880
our cleaning staff [BREAK IN AUDIO] our patient&nbsp;
journeys, and I just want to say thank you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:30.560
all of our staff, again, across health&nbsp;
and social care. Thank you, David.&nbsp;

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:37.840
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Ruth. Can we&nbsp;
move on to the finances, Julian? Would you like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.840 --> 00:41:42.000
to tell us where we are?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:46.720
So, in the notes, in the paper that’s come round&nbsp;
the board, I mean, it basically shows the position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:52.400
to the end of September, which I just noticed&nbsp;
the last month for the kind of retrospective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.400 --> 00:41:57.440
claim process, extra Covid cost we had&nbsp;
in place, it broadly shows that actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:57.440 --> 00:42:03.120
the aggregate level of spend has remained&nbsp;
remarkably consistent over those first six months.&nbsp;

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:08.800
Worth noting, though, that in September, the&nbsp;
kind of Covid costs have continued to come down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:09.520 --> 00:42:13.600
but the kind of, the marginal cost of,&nbsp;
in particular all the extra activity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:17.760
we have put through the acute sector which&nbsp;
Amanda and Pauline had been talking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:25.840
does show that cost ramping up.
Now, for the second half of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.840 --> 00:42:37.360
we have given to the NHS financial envelopes for&nbsp;
them to live within. And that put more money into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.360 --> 00:42:43.840
the NHS, I mean, to reflect the fact that we’re&nbsp;
