00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:36.880
that programme and actually how it fits with some&nbsp;
of the additional investments in technology that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:36.880 --> 00:44:44.160
has also been identified, to be worked out across&nbsp;
the piece what the priorities are for investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:49.520
in the NHS. And I think we just now need to plan&nbsp;
and be clear with government. We are planning on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:53.600
multi-year programme because that’s now the&nbsp;
only sensible way in which we can work out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:59.440
you know, what are the first steps that&nbsp;
we should take this year to realising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:05.040
the vision set out in Mike’s report.
And I think they’ve at least acknowledged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.040 --> 00:45:09.040
at least in the conversations we’ve had, they&nbsp;
recognise this is the beginning of a multi-year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.760 --> 00:45:14.640
multi-year programme. Clearly, we’ll have&nbsp;
to wait until another spending review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:14.640 --> 00:45:18.800
no doubt sometime next summer.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So I mean, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:24.160
Laura, looking at Laura Wade-Gery now,&nbsp;
just in terms of the future, getting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.680 --> 00:45:29.600
digital transformation through the NHS&nbsp;
is going to be so important to getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:36.800
improvements in both quality of care and in&nbsp;
sort of better value if you like. I mean, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:41.520
do you see digital going forwards,&nbsp;
Laura, as part of this capital programme?&nbsp;

00:45:41.520 --> 00:45:47.120
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: Well, I think Julian has&nbsp;
probably put his finger on it in the sense that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:56.560
I think what will really help is when we look at&nbsp;
the funding in the round and examine how we build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:56.560 --> 00:46:03.440
digital into that to maximise the impact&nbsp;
of our spend, because I think we need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.440 --> 00:46:09.680
judge both the short and the long term in here.&nbsp;
And as a number of people have talked about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:10.640 --> 00:46:16.400
what’s been achieved during Covid, in terms of&nbsp;
the sort of shortening of lines of command and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:20.720
things being able to be achieved more quickly.&nbsp;
That’s the kind of thing we’ve got to hang on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:21.360 --> 00:46:28.960
as we develop digitally enabled solutions. But I&nbsp;
think that, that the, you’re right, that playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:28.960 --> 00:46:34.160
the capital into this is going to be key.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Laura. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:35.120 --> 00:46:40.880
innovation is going to be crucial to our future&nbsp;
as well, which sort of leads us on to the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:40.880 --> 00:46:48.160
item. And I see Emily is has stepped up to join us&nbsp;
again, who has executive director line management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.160 --> 00:46:53.360
for the accelerated access collaborative. But I&nbsp;
wonder Ara, did you want to just introduce this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:53.360 --> 00:46:57.213
this next section? Is Matt Whitty here? Is&nbsp;
he here now? I know we’re five minutes ahead.&nbsp;

00:46:57.213 --> 00:46:59.920
&gt;&gt;Laure Wade-Gery: Yes, he’s here.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Did you just want to sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:00.480 --> 00:47:06.240
sketch out a little bit of background, Ara, first?
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Thank you. So thank you very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:11.360
chair, I think everyone in the room will know,&nbsp;
including being in the public board, I’ll give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.360 --> 00:47:14.960
a little bit of summary of what the accelerated&nbsp;
access collaborative is. It’s an attempt by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:14.960 --> 00:47:20.480
government by bringing a number of stakeholders&nbsp;
involved in innovation. Obviously, NHS England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:20.480 --> 00:47:28.640
being the commissioners, NHSI being the providers&nbsp;
of care, and obviously, NICE, the regulator, MHRA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.360 --> 00:47:37.040
and obviously the public, public representatives,&nbsp;
patient representative groups, and, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.040 --> 00:47:42.560
National Institute of Clinical Health Excellence.&nbsp;
And it’s a convening board, really identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.560 --> 00:47:48.720
the high impact innovations that have already&nbsp;
had approval from NICE to accelerate their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.720 --> 00:47:54.480
uptake across the NHS. And it’s been active for&nbsp;
the last two years. And we’re delighted to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.480 --> 00:48:00.240
Matt Whitty with us, who’s the current acting CEO&nbsp;
of the AAC, who’s going to present some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:01.440 --> 00:48:09.200
outputs from the AAC over the last 18 months&nbsp;
or so. And as we all know, AAC is hosted by NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:16.240
England, very grateful for all the executive teams&nbsp;
that have provided support for the success of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:23.280
AAC. Thank you. I’ll hand over to Matt.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Welcome, Matt. I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:26.320
lost you again. Are you there, Matt?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Whitty: Thanks. Thanks, David. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:26.320 --> 00:48:33.520
thanks, thanks, Ara. And thank you all for your&nbsp;
time this afternoon. I won’t cover too much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.520 --> 00:48:39.280
background as I think Lord Darzi’s covered that&nbsp;
sufficiently and most of it will be in the paper.&nbsp;

00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:43.760
Today, I really want to focus on the impact&nbsp;
of our work in the first year of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.760 --> 00:48:48.720
was our expanded role of the accelerated access&nbsp;
collaborative. I’m going to talk a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:48.720 --> 00:48:54.480
about our support in responding to the Covid-19&nbsp;
pandemic. And then I’m going to briefly summarise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:58.240
where we’re going next and ask for a steer on how&nbsp;
best to involve you and your teams on that work.&nbsp;

00:48:59.360 --> 00:49:02.480
So I really wanted to start with&nbsp;
the impacts of what it is the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.200 --> 00:49:08.960
has led to. So we’ve got a range of programmes&nbsp;
that support ideas. And this is right from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:08.960 --> 00:49:14.000
research or early stage innovations right through&nbsp;
to later stage proven or NICE approved innovations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:14.000 --> 00:49:20.320
that are ready for widespread adoption in the NHS.
And in 19/20, around three quarters of a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:24.240
patients benefited from our programmes. So&nbsp;
you can really see the reach of what it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:24.240 --> 00:49:27.440
that we’re achieving here. And I think&nbsp;
that’s really testament to all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:27.440 --> 00:49:32.880
partners and the efforts that are from all of the&nbsp;
colleagues that are involved in the AAC itself.&nbsp;

00:49:33.920 --> 00:49:38.800
Because of the programmes that we supported,&nbsp;
patients spent over 100,000 fewer days in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.800 --> 00:49:44.720
hospital. We supported over 2700 individual&nbsp;
innovations. And many of these were through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:48.400
our delivery partners in the academic health&nbsp;
science networks who are key partners in how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.400 --> 00:49:53.440
we deliver a lot of these innovations, and how we&nbsp;
interface with NHS organisations at a local level.&nbsp;

00:49:54.240 --> 00:50:00.640
We supported a similar number of innovators,&nbsp;
including 150 NHS staff, as part of our clinical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.640 --> 00:50:05.520
entrepreneurs programme. This is a programme&nbsp;
that provides mentoring and guidance to NHS staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:09.040
who have innovative ideas but need support&nbsp;
in developing them. And they can get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:09.040 --> 00:50:14.880
mentoring they require in order to develop&nbsp;
those ideas whilst remaining in the NHS.&nbsp;

00:50:15.920 --> 00:50:21.840
Our programmes saved the NHS at least £50 million&nbsp;
in 19/20. And they also safeguarded or created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:21.840 --> 00:50:27.200
over 1000 jobs in the life sciences sector.
And I’d like to just dig a little deeper into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:27.200 --> 00:50:32.480
a couple of the technologies that we supported&nbsp;
as well. So firstly, placental growth factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:32.480 --> 00:50:37.120
based testing. So this is a NICE approved&nbsp;
test that we supported. And we supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:37.120 --> 00:50:42.160
it through the innovation and technology payment&nbsp;
programme. As I said, it’s a NICE approved test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:42.160 --> 00:50:45.680
and it rules out preeclampsia, which is&nbsp;
a potentially life-threatening condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:45.680 --> 00:50:51.200
in pregnant women. So before our support, this&nbsp;
diagnostic was available in only one hospital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.200 --> 00:50:57.200
At the end of 19/20, over 21,000 pregnant women&nbsp;
in 60 hospitals across England had had access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:57.200 --> 00:51:02.720
that treatment. That’s continued into this year,&nbsp;
and we’ve had over 16,000 pregnant women who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:03.280 --> 00:51:08.240
had access to it, and it’s now in 86 sites in&nbsp;
the first six months of this financial year.&nbsp;

00:51:09.360 --> 00:51:14.400
The second technology I wanted to briefly touch&nbsp;
on is HeartFlow. So this is a NICE approved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:18.960
digital technology that provides information to&nbsp;
clinicians on the flow of blood through the heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:24.640
and its use can help reduce the need for more&nbsp;
invasive procedures. So there are now 50 trusts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.640 --> 00:51:30.080
utilising this technology across England. And&nbsp;
we’re well ahead of European countries. And on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:30.080 --> 00:51:35.040
a per capita basis, we’re about, we’ve got&nbsp;
about 10 times greater uptake than the USA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:40.080
which is where this technology originated from.
So two really good examples of how the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:40.080 --> 00:51:46.240
can adopt innovation at scale, through&nbsp;
the support that we’ve been seeing.&nbsp;

00:51:46.240 --> 00:51:50.720
Last year, we also launched the artificial&nbsp;
intelligence in health and care award. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:50.720 --> 00:51:56.800
is a programme where we directly fund the most&nbsp;
promising artificial intelligence technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:56.800 --> 00:52:02.320
and we evaluate them in NHS settings. And we’re&nbsp;
supporting 42 innovations across 70 sites in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:02.320 --> 00:52:07.840
the UK. The second round is also open, so we’re&nbsp;
looking for more integrations that we can support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:07.840 --> 00:52:13.200
And that closes on the eighth of December.
Secondly, I want to talk about our support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.760 --> 00:52:20.160
during the Covid 19 pandemic. So one of the key&nbsp;
things that we did was that we redeployed our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:23.920
horizon scanning function. So this is our function&nbsp;
where we’re constantly looking for what are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:23.920 --> 00:52:28.080
new technologies, the new medicines that are&nbsp;
coming through that are potentially useful, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.080 --> 00:52:33.840
we redeployed that function to work with others&nbsp;
across NHS England and NHS Improvement, NICE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:38.800
the Medicines and Healthcare Regulatory Agency and&nbsp;
the National Institute for Health Research. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:38.800 --> 00:52:43.840
created something called the research to access&nbsp;
pathway for investigational drugs in Covid-19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:43.840 --> 00:52:49.840
or rapid C-19. This was a process that was&nbsp;
running scans to undertake, looking across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:49.840 --> 00:52:55.280
all the databases to see what are the potentially&nbsp;
beneficial treatments that are coming through that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:55.280 --> 00:53:03.440
could support the efforts for treating Covid. And&nbsp;
this process supported remdesivir, dexamethasone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:03.440 --> 00:53:09.200
and hydrocortisone as being available on the NHS&nbsp;
within hours of the study readouts. So that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:13.840
really, really rapid, compared to what the status&nbsp;
quo would have been without that piece of work.&nbsp;

00:53:15.920 --> 00:53:21.760
So in terms of where we go next, before we move on&nbsp;
to that I just wanted to quickly touch on just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:21.760 --> 00:53:25.840
highlight ready to my team is really committed&nbsp;
to supporting our Black, Asian and minority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.840 --> 00:53:31.120
ethnic colleagues and our patients, particularly&nbsp;
following the realisation of the disproportionate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:31.120 --> 00:53:36.560
impact of Covid-19. And we’re also extremely&nbsp;
mindful of the Black Lives Matter movements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:41.840
And every individual in our team has taken an&nbsp;
equality, diversity and inclusiveness objective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.640 --> 00:53:46.480
just to make sure that we are all doing&nbsp;
what we can to help our efforts there.&nbsp;

00:53:47.200 --> 00:53:51.040
We’re also looking at how that can impact on our&nbsp;
programmes of work. So in the first instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:51.040 --> 00:53:54.960
our NHS clinical entrepreneur programme&nbsp;
have co-developed a programme charter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:54.960 --> 00:53:59.920
which places equality, diversity and inclusiveness&nbsp;
principles at the core, and underpin a range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:59.920 --> 00:54:03.120
activity that we take. And we really believe&nbsp;
that that will actually strengthen the programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:03.120 --> 00:54:07.520
offering for all. And that programme is going&nbsp;
to act as an exemplar about how we inform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:14.080
our future programmes, and how we can embed that&nbsp;
into our programmes to reduce health inequalities.&nbsp;

00:54:15.520 --> 00:54:20.640
Thinking about research, we’ve seen the impacts,&nbsp;
as I mentioned, for the Covid research, so we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:25.440
seen world class Covid research being undertaken&nbsp;
in the NHS. And we’ve seen that not only improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:29.600
patient outcomes here, but that’s changed clinical&nbsp;
pathways across the world. So I also wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:29.600 --> 00:54:33.360
thank the thousands of patients who put themselves&nbsp;
forward for that research to help others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:33.360 --> 00:54:37.360
And we’re working closely with colleagues in the&nbsp;
Department of Health and Social Care, the National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:37.360 --> 00:54:41.600
Institute for Health Research to support the safe&nbsp;
restart of non Covid research as well, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:41.600 --> 00:54:45.600
that’s going to be essential to answering the key&nbsp;
questions for health in the coming years as well.&nbsp;

00:54:46.480 --> 00:54:51.760
And finally, I just wanted to quickly touch on an&nbsp;
answer to the question that Lord Darzi posed about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:51.760 --> 00:54:54.480
what we’re doing about some of the changes&nbsp;
that we’ve been in practice that we’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:54.480 --> 00:54:59.520
during Covid. So we’re working really closely&nbsp;
with [inaudible] beneficial change programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:59.520 --> 00:55:03.200
the Department of Health and Social Care, and&nbsp;
we’re looking to understand what are the changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:03.200 --> 00:55:07.760
that have happened during Covid, and how can we&nbsp;
evaluate these, and we’re doing two things. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:07.760 --> 00:55:12.400
firstly, we’ve got an engagement piece where we’re&nbsp;
looking to understand what those changes are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.400 --> 00:55:16.720
how we can prioritise them, and how we can&nbsp;
evaluate them and understand which ones we keep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.720 --> 00:55:20.400
And secondly, we’re looking at how we can link&nbsp;
that into the existing research and innovation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:24.400
infrastructures. So this is not about inventing&nbsp;
new parts of the structure that we can add to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:24.400 --> 00:55:29.360
already fairly complicated landscape. This is&nbsp;
about how we can take the learnings from Covid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:29.360 --> 00:55:33.840
evaluate them thoroughly, and implement&nbsp;
using the existing infrastructure.&nbsp;

00:55:34.800 --> 00:55:38.640
Finally, we’ll use the outputs of that to help&nbsp;
review our priorities. And we’ll take that to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:38.640 --> 00:55:43.120
the AAC board in 2021.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:43.120 --> 00:55:46.160
Thank you very much, Matt. That&nbsp;
was a very good introduction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.360 --> 00:55:50.320
Munir, you’ve got a question?
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: Yes, thank you very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:50.320 --> 00:55:56.000
Matt, really great paper, and really good to&nbsp;
see the impact of the AAC. I wonder whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:56.000 --> 00:56:01.280
you can give a broad overview of what you see&nbsp;
are the major challenges in terms of getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:01.280 --> 00:56:08.160
innovation into the NHS? Is it organisational? Is&nbsp;
it behavioural, is it funding? So, what needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:08.160 --> 00:56:12.800
be done to be able to upscale what you’re doing&nbsp;
in terms of improving the uptake of innovations?&nbsp;

00:56:12.800 --> 00:56:18.080
&gt;&gt;Matthew Whitty: Well, all of – thank&nbsp;
you very much, all, all of the above,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:18.080 --> 00:56:22.160
as you say, funding, so we’ve seen with through&nbsp;
our innovation and technology payment programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:22.960 --> 00:56:27.360
that actually where we centrally fund technologies&nbsp;
that can help to support the uptake of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:27.360 --> 00:56:32.720
technologies, but it’s not, it’s certainly not the&nbsp;
only answer. And even when we are directly funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:32.720 --> 00:56:36.960
technologies, they don’t always get the uptake&nbsp;
that that we would like because of other factors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:37.520 --> 00:56:42.640
So a lot of it comes down to how strong the&nbsp;
clinical case is. These things need to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:42.640 --> 00:56:48.160
clinical support. And we always encourage the&nbsp;
innovators that we talk to, to engage with NICE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.160 --> 00:56:53.040
at an early stage and to take their products&nbsp;
through NICE in order to get that sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:54.640 --> 00:56:59.680
robust clinical consensus. And it’s much easier&nbsp;
for NICE to have that consensus than it is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:02.960
us to have those at a national level without&nbsp;
the infrastructure that they’ve got there.&nbsp;

00:57:03.600 --> 00:57:08.880
I think secondly, there really needs to be a&nbsp;
clear case that patients can benefit. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:08.880 --> 00:57:14.560
we really need to do more to get that pull from&nbsp;
patients who are demanding these technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:14.560 --> 00:57:18.880
in order to drive that culture change.
And I think in terms of the culture change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:18.880 --> 00:57:22.480
I think there’s a lot more that&nbsp;
we can do to change the culture of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:22.480 --> 00:57:27.920
innovation in the NHS. Probably&nbsp;
the key thing that we see is where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:27.920 --> 00:57:31.920
and we saw this with a piece of work that we did&nbsp;
with the quantitative [inaudible] where we went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:31.920 --> 00:57:36.000
round to a range of providers who we could see&nbsp;
that were extremely good at adopting innovation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:36.000 --> 00:57:39.840
And we spoke to them about what is it that makes&nbsp;
them particularly good at adopting innovation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:41.040 --> 00:57:45.200
And one of the things that differentiated the&nbsp;
hospitals that were very good at it from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:45.200 --> 00:57:49.440
hospitals that were less good at it was&nbsp;
actually they were not just focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:50.560 --> 00:57:55.440
creating new inventions and new innovations and&nbsp;
problem solving, because we saw that widespread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:55.440 --> 00:58:01.040
across the board. It was where they could actively&nbsp;
look for innovations and solutions in other areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:06.000
so from other providers. They weren’t just&nbsp;
concerned about sort of promoting and progressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:11.600
their own technologies. Secondly, when they had&nbsp;
those, they had board level support, so somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:11.600 --> 00:58:17.920
where they could actually take that to and that&nbsp;
person or that group was empowered by the board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:17.920 --> 00:58:22.480
to actually implement those and to plug into the&nbsp;
clinical teams and the clinical networks that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:22.480 --> 00:58:28.080
required, and to link that into their contractual&nbsp;
changes in order to make sure that that that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:28.080 --> 00:58:29.960
transacted smoothly.
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:30.960 --> 00:58:38.880
Prior: Thanks, Matt. Tim, then David and Ara.
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: Thank you very much. I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:38.880 --> 00:58:45.360
wanted to comment that from my side of the&nbsp;
Atlantic, the efforts that you are putting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:47.040 --> 00:58:54.720
are really remarkable. The fact that on my side of&nbsp;
the Atlantic, we allow technology adoption to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:54.720 --> 00:59:01.680
a really completely haphazard process, and&nbsp;
the fact that you have an organised effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:01.680 --> 00:59:10.800
as detailed through this excellent paper, I found&nbsp;
insightful, remarkable, and, frankly, I’m envious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:11.520 --> 00:59:20.880
So kudos to you and your team and, and for the&nbsp;
whole, the effort to move these technologies.&nbsp;

00:59:22.160 --> 00:59:28.160
On my side of the Atlantic we call it the&nbsp;
fourth translational block, is the academic term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:28.160 --> 00:59:35.280
for this, and it often is the difference&nbsp;
between a technology getting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:35.920 --> 00:59:44.160
help patients and not, and having an organised&nbsp;
effort to address the fourth transitional block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:44.160 --> 00:59:54.560
is terrific so thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:54.560 --> 00:59:58.453
David, I think you’re on mute, so I’m&nbsp;
guessing, lip-reading that you were.&nbsp;

00:59:58.453 --> 01:00:04.160
&gt;&gt;David Behan [?]: So Matthew, great stuff.&nbsp;
Thank you. It’s really just a word of caution&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:05.600 --> 01:00:11.920
on artificial intelligence, and I’m sure&nbsp;
it’s well plugged in. But I would – the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:11.920 --> 01:00:16.800
it has incredible power. Of course, it&nbsp;
covers many different spectrums of activity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:17.760 --> 01:00:25.040
But the, when thinking about awarding, I&nbsp;
would have a screen that says, can people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:25.040 --> 01:00:30.720
show you, demonstrate to you how&nbsp;
they are ensuring there is no bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:31.360 --> 01:00:39.920
in the work. Because there is an increasing degree&nbsp;
of evidence, I think, that there is implicit bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:39.920 --> 01:00:44.480
based on the fact of the data or the&nbsp;
algorithm, etc, that they’re sort of using.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.440 --> 01:00:49.440
I just sort of I don’t know what we’re thinking&nbsp;
about that. But it’s quite prevalent and can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:49.440 --> 01:00:55.200
really quite profound.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:59.440
Thanks, David. And Ara, did you&nbsp;
have? Your hand was up a minute ago.&nbsp;

01:00:59.440 --> 01:01:04.240
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Yeah, just to finish off to thank&nbsp;
Matt for a remarkable job he’s done. He just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.240 --> 01:01:10.560
pointed on one thing, the most important part&nbsp;
of this after fixing all the national issues is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:14.240
patients. It’s patient empowerment, and&nbsp;
it’s really bringing patients with us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:14.800 --> 01:01:21.520
In my own personal experience, you know, most&nbsp;
of my services, and what I provide has changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.520 --> 01:01:26.480
because of a patient pull. And that’s absolutely,&nbsp;
and obviously, it has to be evidence based. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:26.480 --> 01:01:32.320
just to finish off, I just want to say thanks&nbsp;
to NHS England, the old executive, Simon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:33.280 --> 01:01:39.840
and many others who’ve really been, Julian,&nbsp;
who looks at the prices of these things and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:39.840 --> 01:01:43.760
the cost of these things, you know, they’ve&nbsp;
been exceptionally generous in their support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:43.760 --> 01:01:47.920
and making this to get this to what it is. But&nbsp;
this has been a chronic problem in the past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:48.800 --> 01:01:53.200
And we’ve managed to get through some of&nbsp;
these difficulties. Thanks to you all.&nbsp;

01:01:53.200 --> 01:01:57.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think it’s worth&nbsp;
saying as well, Ara, and actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:58.560 --> 01:02:05.360
really coming back on Tim’s point that compared to&nbsp;
the US, it may be more haphazard in the US. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:05.360 --> 01:02:12.800
you know, the US does adopt innovation to a much&nbsp;
greater degree much quicker than the UK does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:12.800 --> 01:02:17.360
And I think what the AAC have done is started&nbsp;
to rectify some of that. But we have actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:17.360 --> 01:02:24.320
got a long way to go. It is incredibly difficult&nbsp;
to get adoption taken up at speed and at scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:24.960 --> 01:02:29.280
within the NHS. So I think, but I mean,&nbsp;
Matt, I think you’re doing a fabulous job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:29.280 --> 01:02:32.880
you know that. I mean, I don’t think we would have&nbsp;
got the Grail thing done without your support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:33.520 --> 01:02:38.960
The fact that you’re working so closely with&nbsp;
Novartis on the Inclisiran adoption in the UK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:39.760 --> 01:02:44.800
the work you’re now doing on polygenic risk scores&nbsp;
and clinical trials is all you know, fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:44.800 --> 01:02:50.800
stuff. So you know, all power to your elbow.
And I think Ara, you’ve given the AAC a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:50.800 --> 01:02:57.200
lift by working so closely with it. So thanks to&nbsp;
you as well. Emily, did you want to have a final&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:57.200 --> 01:03:01.040
– you’ve joined us, did you want to&nbsp;
have a final word on this, Emily?&nbsp;

01:03:01.040 --> 01:03:07.680
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: I would just add thanks to&nbsp;
the team, and I think particularly to also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:07.680 --> 01:03:14.240
as Ara said to Julian’s team, because some of&nbsp;
the, you know, the complexities of evaluating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:14.240 --> 01:03:18.800
ensuring continuity of funding so that we&nbsp;
don’t interrupt patient care to Ara’s point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:18.800 --> 01:03:23.440
was particularly to some of the devices pieces,&nbsp;
has been incredibly important. So massive joint&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:23.440 --> 01:03:27.120
effort, and of course, working across government&nbsp;
as well, not just with NHS England. So to thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:27.120 --> 01:03:31.120
everybody for the support and Matt for&nbsp;
his and his team’s really impressive work.&nbsp;

01:03:31.120 --> 01:03:36.000
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Well, Matt, thank&nbsp;
you very much for joining us. Thank you.&nbsp;

01:03:37.520 --> 01:03:43.040
The next item is integrated care systems, which&nbsp;
Simon is going to introduce. I think actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:43.040 --> 01:03:47.280
Simon, you sort of introduced this when&nbsp;
you first wrote the five year forward view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:48.080 --> 01:03:52.800
which is, you know, seven years ago. So that’s&nbsp;
when you started testing out the sort of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:52.800 --> 01:03:58.400
concept of integrated care system. So&nbsp;
would you like to introduce this paper?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:02.560 --> 01:04:05.280
You’re on mute Simon.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:05.280 --> 01:04:09.920
David. I will do briefly then I’m going to&nbsp;
hand over to Amanda. I think as you say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:09.920 --> 01:04:13.360
the first point to note is that we’ve been on&nbsp;
this journey for some while so this is not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:13.360 --> 01:04:18.480
blank sheet of paper. This is not a tabula&nbsp;
rasa. This is essentially setting out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:18.480 --> 01:04:24.400
the next steps for April 2021 needs to&nbsp;
look like with or without legislation.&nbsp;

01:04:25.120 --> 01:04:31.040
However, on the legislative point, it’s worth&nbsp;
also saying that you’ll recall that we had widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:31.680 --> 01:04:37.760
consulted on our proposals previously. So again,&nbsp;
this is not starting from scratch. These are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:38.560 --> 01:04:42.880
a couple of sort of evolutionary updates&nbsp;
to the proposals that have been through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:42.880 --> 01:04:48.480
the NHS assembly and have got, I think, a wide&nbsp;
support across the service and with our partners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:48.480 --> 01:04:53.600
but let me hand over to Amanda and then perhaps&nbsp;
following discussion, we can come back to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:53.600 --> 01:04:57.760
of the next steps on the legislative point.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And I think Bill McCarthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:57.760 --> 01:05:00.720
has joined us on this call Amanda as&nbsp;
well, you might like to introduce him.&nbsp;

01:05:00.720 --> 01:05:08.240
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you, Simon&nbsp;
and David. Yes. I’ve got Bill McCarthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:08.240 --> 01:05:13.360
who is the regional director for the Northwest, is&nbsp;
with us for this part of the discussion. Bill has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:13.360 --> 01:05:18.640
led an awful lot of the work on the development of&nbsp;
this policy and been involved in some of the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:18.640 --> 01:05:22.880
deep engagement that’s gone on over the past&nbsp;
few weeks and months to get us to this point.&nbsp;

01:05:22.880 --> 01:05:26.480
So I’d be delighted, David, if I just&nbsp;
say a few words, maybe to hand over to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:26.480 --> 01:05:30.560
Bill just to add to what I’m saying, and&nbsp;
of course, to answer questions as well.&nbsp;

01:05:31.280 --> 01:05:37.360
So just to pick up on what Simon said, by way&nbsp;
of introduction, I think it is, it’s right that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:37.360 --> 01:05:44.080
this proposal today is as much about next year&nbsp;
as it is about potentially where we might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:44.080 --> 01:05:49.840
from April 22 onwards, because this does really&nbsp;
reflect I think what we have consistently heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:50.560 --> 01:05:57.280
from colleagues across the NHS, local government,&nbsp;
wider partners over the last weeks and months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:57.280 --> 01:06:03.040
which is that there is a real commitment to going&nbsp;
further on the integrated care journey. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:03.040 --> 01:06:09.520
is a call from, I think, our partners to us to&nbsp;
make that easier. And some of what’s contained in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:09.520 --> 01:06:15.920
the document is really trying then just to say how&nbsp;
do we then remove some of the barriers that exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:15.920 --> 01:06:21.520
at the moment and how do we assist people to do&nbsp;
in the main what they want to do anyway, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:21.520 --> 01:06:26.400
I think this is the first point to make.
The real point of all of this is four&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:26.400 --> 01:06:32.160
things. It is about an absolute commitment to&nbsp;
improving population health and health care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:32.160 --> 01:06:38.880
tackling unequal outcomes and access, enhancing&nbsp;
productivity and value for money, and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:38.880 --> 01:06:42.640
something that’s been writ large&nbsp;
through the Covid experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:42.640 --> 01:06:49.120
it is about helping the NHS to support the&nbsp;
broader social and economic development, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:49.120 --> 01:06:56.560
the need to find a way of being able to&nbsp;
organise ourselves around those priorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:56.560 --> 01:07:01.440
is the thing that is driving this work.
So it starts with purpose. As I say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:01.440 --> 01:07:05.520
it’s a deepening really of, and a&nbsp;
next step of a journey that we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:05.520 --> 01:07:10.960
been on for some time. And it’s about removing&nbsp;
barriers that enable us to take those steps.&nbsp;

01:07:10.960 --> 01:07:19.600
So specifically, what the document describes is&nbsp;
a commitment, I think now to saying we know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:19.600 --> 01:07:27.440
decisions taken closest to the communities that&nbsp;
they affect are likely to lead to better outcomes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:27.440 --> 01:07:33.360
So that absolute importance of the neighbourhood,&nbsp;
of the local community and organising services&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:33.360 --> 01:07:38.960
and empowering decision making at that level.
Secondly, the collaboration between partners&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:38.960 --> 01:07:44.400
at place. So that’s across health and&nbsp;
all aspects of health, social care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:44.960 --> 01:07:50.480
wider local government, voluntary sector,&nbsp;
patients themselves, of course, at place level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:51.040 --> 01:07:58.240
again, is where we see the greatest opportunity to&nbsp;
make the biggest impact on the health and outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:58.240 --> 01:08:03.680
of the population. And we know that about 80% of&nbsp;
healthcare takes place at that local place level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:09.600
so the ability now to start to think about how&nbsp;
do we effectively devolve funding to that level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:09.600 --> 01:08:14.400
how do we set up governance structures that binds&nbsp;
in partners to be able to work effectively at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:14.400 --> 01:08:18.960
level, building on the journey that we’ve been&nbsp;
on so far is part of what this document talks to.&nbsp;

01:08:18.960 --> 01:08:23.840
But then there is a third aspect, which is&nbsp;
a broader collaboration between providers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.400 --> 01:08:28.240
some of which will need to take place at a much&nbsp;
wider geographical level, because if we want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:28.240 --> 01:08:34.640
start to get into being able to do things like&nbsp;
approach recovery with a real eye to equalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:34.640 --> 01:08:38.240
if we want to be able to really deploy mutual&nbsp;
aid in the way we’ve done this year, as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:38.240 --> 01:08:42.240
more part of our business as usual operating,&nbsp;
if we want to be able to start getting into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:42.240 --> 01:08:48.400
specialist services redesign and specialist&nbsp;
services provision, then a wider geographical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.400 --> 01:08:54.080
basis will make much more sense for that.
So what we said in the document is ICSs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:54.080 --> 01:08:58.400
really need to be able to be organised to do&nbsp;
a number of things. They need to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:58.400 --> 01:09:03.200
do the distribution of financial resources, and&nbsp;
it includes the improvement and transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:03.200 --> 01:09:07.920
resource have the levers around operational&nbsp;
delivery, and that includes of course, workforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:07.920 --> 01:09:13.280
planning, commissioning development, emergency&nbsp;
planning response, and crucially, the digital and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.280 --> 01:09:18.400
data architecture to enable them to do that.
So if that’s what we’re asking of ICSs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:18.400 --> 01:09:23.760
as we go forward, what needs to be true?
So what we’ve said in this document, and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:23.760 --> 01:09:28.400
when I say we, I mean, this has really been&nbsp;
co-created so deeply with partners across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:28.400 --> 01:09:37.360
system that it feels like, it’s, it’s slightly odd&nbsp;
saying we in some ways as we describe it, but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:37.360 --> 01:09:44.880
we have said in this document is there is a very&nbsp;
specific set here of opportunities for providers.&nbsp;

01:09:45.440 --> 01:09:49.440
So there’s an opportunity here to&nbsp;
have a very clear seat at the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:50.240 --> 01:09:56.720
be part of that decision making at ICS level&nbsp;
but then to take responsibility in partnership&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:56.720 --> 01:10:02.160
for delivering some of those outcomes and have&nbsp;
the levers and the responsibility to do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:02.880 --> 01:10:08.880
which we know that many places have self-organised&nbsp;
to do, despite some of the barriers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:08.880 --> 01:10:15.360
but this allows hopefully this to be kind of&nbsp;
locked in now as a way of working for next year.&nbsp;

01:10:15.920 --> 01:10:20.320
So higher quality, more sustainable services&nbsp;
and providers at the table to make those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:20.320 --> 01:10:25.120
decisions alongside other partners.
So it is a change, it will be a change&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:25.120 --> 01:10:31.280
for some providers, but in the main a change&nbsp;
that we know that ¡ our provider colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:31.840 --> 01:10:35.440
are looking for, and looking for the&nbsp;
opportunity to contribute in this way.&nbsp;

01:10:36.720 --> 01:10:43.840
So the other big kind of offer here, I&nbsp;
suppose, is really to local government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:43.840 --> 01:10:49.680
where what we’re saying is, the whole point,&nbsp;
and Simon wrote it right back when we did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:49.680 --> 01:10:55.040
long term plan and before that, in the five&nbsp;
year forward view, the whole point of this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:55.040 --> 01:10:59.120
if we are going to be able to deliver the&nbsp;
kind of improvements to population health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:59.120 --> 01:11:03.200
that this is all about, then we’ve got to&nbsp;
do it in partnership. And there’s a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:03.200 --> 01:11:08.800
very obvious opportunity, not just at ICS level,&nbsp;
but particularly at place level, to bring that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.800 --> 01:11:14.320
to life. And we’ve seen that across this year in&nbsp;
the response to Covid. So each area is different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.320 --> 01:11:22.160
but what we’ve tried to do in this document is&nbsp;
say, welcome, we need, we encourage flexibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:22.160 --> 01:11:27.680
but within a framework. And the framework is the&nbsp;
thing that allows us to then give confidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:27.680 --> 01:11:33.040
particularly to local government partners, about&nbsp;
what that seat at the table looks like, and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.040 --> 01:11:40.800
they will play that critical partnership role&nbsp;
with the NHS in delivering that population health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:40.800 --> 01:11:46.800
ambition, and that wider set of objectives around&nbsp;
the social and economic recovery of the country.&nbsp;

01:11:47.760 --> 01:11:53.600
So I won’t go through every single bit of this, I&nbsp;
could, but I won’t. Instead, I think I’d just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:53.600 --> 01:11:59.920
to draw out a couple of things, if I may. Because&nbsp;
the third group of people who will read this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:59.920 --> 01:12:05.600
and I think, see potentially something very&nbsp;
exciting in it, but also let’s be honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:05.600 --> 01:12:11.760
something that’s also potentially daunting, are&nbsp;
our commissioning colleagues, because what we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.760 --> 01:12:16.960
describing here is a very clear and important&nbsp;
role for commissioning, but an evolution from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.960 --> 01:12:23.280
where we are now. So into something that’s much&nbsp;
more about strategic commissioning at ICS level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.280 --> 01:12:29.120
about helping really drive some of those changes,&nbsp;
improvements in pathways at place level, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:29.120 --> 01:12:35.280
something that gets right alongside the providers&nbsp;
so that we’re driving again, change that’s for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:35.280 --> 01:12:41.760
the benefit of population. But we’re describing&nbsp;
that in a way which I think means that although&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:41.760 --> 01:12:47.120
that highly values the role of commissioning&nbsp;
colleagues, that will by necessity look a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:47.120 --> 01:12:51.680
little different to where we are at the moment,&nbsp;
it will be that evolutionary step on the journey.&nbsp;

01:12:52.960 --> 01:12:56.960
So just to say, and the document sets&nbsp;
it out, I hope, reasonably clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:56.960 --> 01:13:04.000
but there are certain things that we’ve described&nbsp;
in here that kind of are I think necessary, but on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.000 --> 01:13:09.920
their own insufficient, but in totality, hopefully&nbsp;
add up to the underpinning things that need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:09.920 --> 01:13:14.720
true to allow the steps to be taken next year.
It is about a financial framework that supports&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:14.720 --> 01:13:20.240
this way of working. It is crucially about this&nbsp;
clarity on digital and data and the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:20.240 --> 01:13:24.720
that needs to come together at system level.&nbsp;
It’s a recognition that regulation also needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:24.720 --> 01:13:29.200
to change and the way that we describe our&nbsp;
single oversight framework needs to reflect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:29.200 --> 01:13:36.160
what it means to be an individual organisation&nbsp;
playing a role as now in the system, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.160 --> 01:13:40.720
what it means to be an effective player in&nbsp;
partnership with other system colleagues.&nbsp;

01:13:41.520 --> 01:13:46.720
It talks about, as I say, the way commissioning&nbsp;
will change in the way that I’ve just said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:46.720 --> 01:13:51.520
and a bit about the key principles that we’ll need&nbsp;
to guide us as we think about the next steps also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:51.520 --> 01:13:54.800
in relation to specialist commissioning.
And then finally as Simon says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:55.520 --> 01:14:00.800
it does set out two different options around&nbsp;
legislation. Ian might want to talk a bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:00.800 --> 01:14:05.760
about this, he’s helped shape the thinking&nbsp;
on this over the last again few months-&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:05.760 --> 01:14:10.640
It moves on from where we were with legislation&nbsp;
before where we were talking, I mean much of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:11.200 --> 01:14:17.280
of course, the same and a very clear build on&nbsp;
it. But does take us forward to talking about two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:17.280 --> 01:14:24.480
options, one of which is the statutory committee&nbsp;
model, the other a statutory corporate NHS body.&nbsp;

01:14:25.760 --> 01:14:31.840
Describes in next steps, I think a very&nbsp;
clear and from my point of view, a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:31.840 --> 01:14:37.680
important commitment to saying given that this is&nbsp;
something which feels like it’s a very bottom up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:37.680 --> 01:14:44.800
driven set of changes, we need in the way&nbsp;
that we manage next steps, if we do end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:46.320 --> 01:14:51.040
really adopting this and going for it and going&nbsp;
for it quickly, to think really carefully about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.040 --> 01:15:00.480
how we support our people through this. So a&nbsp;
minimal disruption model, a real commitment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:00.480 --> 01:15:07.920
to that continued employment promise for anyone&nbsp;
whose roles are disrupted through this process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:13.920
And that is, really, I think, an important&nbsp;
principle for us to hold on to through this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:13.920 --> 01:15:21.120
because I’m conscious that while for most people,&nbsp;
I hope you read this, they will see something in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:21.120 --> 01:15:25.840
it to get very excited about, we also need to&nbsp;
recognise that change is difficult, and that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:25.840 --> 01:15:30.960
need to support people through that because not&nbsp;
everybody will be perhaps quite as enthusiastic.&nbsp;

01:15:30.960 --> 01:15:35.600
So really important that this is an engagement&nbsp;
document, we’re looking for feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:35.600 --> 01:15:41.680
we’re seeking now views not just from the NHS, but&nbsp;
of course from a much wider set of stakeholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:41.680 --> 01:15:47.200
and broader partners over the next six weeks&nbsp;
to help us really then hone down and shape not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:47.200 --> 01:15:51.520
just our recommendations to government from a&nbsp;
legislation point of view, but also to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:51.520 --> 01:15:57.600
the sort of further thinking, the detail behind&nbsp;
what we do next year to really get some momentum&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:57.600 --> 01:16:02.160
behind this and make it make a difference on&nbsp;
the ground, because that’s what it’s all about.&nbsp;

01:16:02.960 --> 01:16:07.200
So I’ll stop there. But Ian I know will want&nbsp;
to come in and [inaudible] has his hand up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:07.200 --> 01:16:12.240
but David, Bill might want to add as well.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Just before we bring Ian in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:13.120 --> 01:16:18.480
Bill is there is here as well, Amanda, I’d just&nbsp;
like to get your reflections on this as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:18.480 --> 01:16:23.040
Bill. You’ve seen, we’ve been talking&nbsp;
about integrated care for many, many years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:23.680 --> 01:16:30.320
and you’ve been sort of involved in the policy for&nbsp;
a long, long time. What are your reflections on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:31.680 --> 01:16:35.680
the proposal before us today?
&gt;&gt;Bill McCarthy: Thanks. Thanks, David.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:37.520 --> 01:16:42.960
I shan’t add to what Amanda’s said, I thought that&nbsp;
was very clear, but perhaps just three points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:43.920 --> 01:16:54.640
David. First one is about the process. I think I&nbsp;
can’t stress too highly really that this isn’t a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:54.640 --> 01:17:05.360
bit of blue sky thinking from, you know, a remote&nbsp;
body somewhere. This really is our understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:05.360 --> 01:17:12.400
of how the best of the NHS and partners locally&nbsp;
have responded to two very significant events.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:12.400 --> 01:17:18.640
One, the long term plan and the challenge that was&nbsp;
set out in that about integration, about working&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:18.640 --> 01:17:25.200
around the needs of patients, but then also&nbsp;
the Covid experience over the past few months.&nbsp;

01:17:26.800 --> 01:17:34.880
What’s in here is drawing from that real&nbsp;
experience of where I think our leading systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:34.880 --> 01:17:40.560
have managed through in a way which has achieved&nbsp;
the four objectives that Amanda set out.&nbsp;

01:17:41.760 --> 01:17:46.880
Second point, I think really important here&nbsp;
is clinical leadership will be and needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:46.880 --> 01:17:54.720
to be written right the way through these new&nbsp;
arrangements. We were talking before finalising&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.720 --> 01:18:01.920
the document with colleagues in the royal&nbsp;
colleges, as well as with the GP leadership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:02.480 --> 01:18:09.840
And I think there is a real appetite&nbsp;
to be fully engaged at place, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:09.840 --> 01:18:16.400
in the provider collaboratives, but also at the&nbsp;
ICS level. And I think it’s really important we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:17.200 --> 01:18:23.840
reinforce that message and make sure the way&nbsp;
this is implemented is you know, by embracing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:24.560 --> 01:18:28.960
that real strength that we’re referring to&nbsp;
earlier, actually, that clinical leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:35.440
will give to the way we can better&nbsp;
shape services to serve our patients.&nbsp;

01:18:36.000 --> 01:18:43.760
And I think the final point I wanted to make is&nbsp;
it’s perhaps a little bit parochial. It’s a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:45.760 --> 01:18:52.400
it’s very important to people in the northwest, if&nbsp;
I may, David, and it’s, it’s about inequalities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:53.360 --> 01:19:00.960
And the, I think Covid has shone really&nbsp;
quite a harsh light on some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:01.920 --> 01:19:08.960
underlying inequalities in health status and&nbsp;
in opportunity, which we’ve kind of known&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:10.160 --> 01:19:15.120
have been there, and which have been,&nbsp;
I think, quite seriously exposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:16.240 --> 01:19:23.680
through Covid. I’m convinced from speaking to&nbsp;
colleagues locally, including local government&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:23.680 --> 01:19:30.640
colleagues, as well as the voluntary sector and&nbsp;
our own people, that this approach to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:31.600 --> 01:19:37.840
deep partnership at the level of place, a real&nbsp;
collective understanding of the highest risks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:37.840 --> 01:19:43.520
of full participation of the NHS in the social&nbsp;
and economic development, as well as in meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:43.520 --> 01:19:50.240
the needs of those most vulnerable will be at the&nbsp;
heart of tackling inequality themes of the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:50.240 --> 01:19:55.760
as well as the opportunity that the&nbsp;
provider collaboratives on a bigger scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:55.760 --> 01:20:01.680
will really give to make sure that access&nbsp;
to services doesn’t just depend on which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:01.680 --> 01:20:07.600
hospital you happen to live closest to. We&nbsp;
can organise that better, and I think we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:07.600 --> 01:20:14.080
do better on the back at this to tackle those&nbsp;
inequalities that I think is our obligation.&nbsp;

01:20:16.960 --> 01:20:23.120
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Bill. Ian.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, David, I just wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:23.120 --> 01:20:28.880
to make two points about legislation. So,&nbsp;
the first builds on Simon and Amanda’s points&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:29.840 --> 01:20:37.280
around this document being what ICSs and systems&nbsp;
can get on with and do in advance of legislation.&nbsp;

01:20:38.080 --> 01:20:46.640
And I think what marks it out from&nbsp;
previous journeys is the extent to which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:47.440 --> 01:20:55.920
we’re seeking to design almost a legislative&nbsp;
commencement moment to have the least possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:55.920 --> 01:21:03.680
practical import. This is not about a lot of&nbsp;
change happening at the date of royal assent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:03.680 --> 01:21:12.320
commencement. It is about facilitating change to&nbsp;
continue to happen, as it has been happening now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:13.120 --> 01:21:18.640
and in advance of that moment, whenever that&nbsp;
is, and indeed thereafter. And I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.640 --> 01:21:27.600
does mark a fundamental difference in approach.&nbsp;
I’m not just looking back to 2012, or 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:27.600 --> 01:21:31.920
actually, but previous changes that&nbsp;
have occurred in the preceding decades.&nbsp;

01:21:32.800 --> 01:21:39.200
And the second point I wanted to highlight&nbsp;
is Amanda’s point around process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.160 --> 01:21:48.000
Last October, we discussed as a board a really&nbsp;
important set of proposals that had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:48.000 --> 01:21:56.720
shaped by people in the NHS. We had a massive&nbsp;
engagement process with nearly 200,000 responses&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:58.400 --> 01:22:05.440
setting out our proposals and recommendations to&nbsp;
government, and they were genuinely shaped through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:05.440 --> 01:22:12.800
a consensus building process. And the reason why&nbsp;
we have come back with these adapted proposals for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:12.800 --> 01:22:19.600
legislative change, specifically in relation&nbsp;
to ICSs, is we had been hearing, thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:20.160 --> 01:22:27.040
the further years’ worth of work in integrated&nbsp;
systems, and also the experience of Covid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:28.640 --> 01:22:37.200
a degree of disquiet in the NHS and our&nbsp;
partners that what we’ve proposed in relation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:37.200 --> 01:22:44.400
legislative support for ICSs may have been too&nbsp;
light and doesn’t meet their full ambition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:44.960 --> 01:22:51.120
And it is in response to that, that we have&nbsp;
worked up these two specific options. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:51.120 --> 01:22:58.960
exactly the same way that we engaged our partners&nbsp;
in a full and transparent and consensual process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:58.960 --> 01:23:06.960
that’s why today marks the launch of an engagement&nbsp;
process publicly. And we commit in exactly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:06.960 --> 01:23:15.440
same way to listen really hard to what people are&nbsp;
saying, and to come back with our final proposals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:16.240 --> 01:23:24.240
revised or otherwise. And we will publicly set&nbsp;
out our own independent advice to government to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:24.240 --> 01:23:31.600
help shape the drafting of any forthcoming bill.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Ian, for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:33.200 --> 01:23:37.200
Tim and then David Behan.
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:37.200 --> 01:23:43.840
Thank you, David. I’d like to just ask for&nbsp;
your indulgence for a moment to state what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:43.840 --> 01:23:50.800
it means to be a provider inside an integrated&nbsp;
system, and how that affects patient care. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:50.800 --> 01:23:58.560
just want to emphasise as a primary care doctor&nbsp;
who has practised basically in solo practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:59.120 --> 01:24:06.320
and then practiced through the transition to&nbsp;
the integrated delivery system, that I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:07.040 --> 01:24:13.200
for our patients is remarkable. And, and&nbsp;
I want to highlight some, just a few of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:13.200 --> 01:24:17.760
the things that are benefits to our patients.
I first want to say that for the patient with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:19.040 --> 01:24:25.680
a minor acute episode, really, this is about&nbsp;
convenience, access and improved experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:26.800 --> 01:24:33.440
But the main benefits actually accrue to&nbsp;
our patients who have chronic illness, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:33.440 --> 01:24:40.880
complex chronic illness. And the way our systems&nbsp;
when they are not coordinated and integrated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:41.600 --> 01:24:49.760
force the patients to be the navigator of their&nbsp;
own care delivery is the burden we put on our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:49.760 --> 01:24:53.520
patients, particularly those with&nbsp;
complex chronic illness. It’s really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:53.520 --> 01:24:59.200
it’s hard to imagine unless you’ve experienced it.
Integrated delivery systems have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:01.920 --> 01:25:08.720
really big benefit for our patients of&nbsp;
actually coordinating that care for them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:08.720 --> 01:25:13.360
because of the very nature of the integration.&nbsp;
I think the term on your side of the pond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:13.360 --> 01:25:19.680
it’s often used is joined up care. So I’ve&nbsp;
experienced that as a provider, a primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:19.680 --> 01:25:26.400
care provider. And I actually can’t imagine going&nbsp;
back to a world in which I wasn’t able to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:26.400 --> 01:25:33.360
that kind of wraparound care to the patients that&nbsp;
I care for. And I am really looking forward to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:35.840 --> 01:25:42.320
helping in any way I can the push to do&nbsp;
this within the National Health Service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:42.320 --> 01:25:46.720
So thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:46.720 --> 01:25:51.280
Thanks very much, Tim. David Behan.
&gt;&gt;David Behan: Thanks. Thanks, David. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:52.640 --> 01:25:58.000
my point might build on the one that Tim’s just&nbsp;
made. I think this is a significant development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:58.000 --> 01:26:05.120
and I absolutely take the point that this is&nbsp;
part of an evolutionary process of change.&nbsp;

01:26:05.840 --> 01:26:11.280
But I just wonder whether in the document itself&nbsp;
and the explanation, whether the voice and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:11.280 --> 01:26:18.480
interest of those people who are using services&nbsp;
has been given enough prominence. I hope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:18.480 --> 01:26:25.200
builds on the point that Tim’s made about where&nbsp;
the advantages are from working in this way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:25.200 --> 01:26:32.480
for people who are using services and their&nbsp;
carers. And as I say, I’ll just reflect whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:32.480 --> 01:26:37.840
we’ve said enough about what the, how the voice of&nbsp;
people will be captured and their interests will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:37.840 --> 01:26:42.560
be captured in the way forward. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Amanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:43.200 --> 01:26:48.800
I might ask you just to sort of respond to David&nbsp;
and Tim’s points there. But in particular, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:49.920 --> 01:26:56.560
in the paper, there is a reference to&nbsp;
putting both digital and data transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:56.560 --> 01:26:59.760
but also putting the citizen at the&nbsp;
centre of their care, which I think really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:00.320 --> 01:27:04.560
goes to the heart of what Tim and David&nbsp;
are getting at. Would you like to sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:04.560 --> 01:27:08.640
just reflect on that?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:08.640 --> 01:27:15.200
Yes, thank you. Look, thank you for that&nbsp;
really helpful feedback into my colleagues for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:15.200 --> 01:27:18.320
for building on and amplifying some of the&nbsp;
things that I said by way of introduction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:18.320 --> 01:27:24.080
So I think it’s, it’s in a sense, the opportunity,&nbsp;
the great opportunity of having this out there as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:24.080 --> 01:27:29.120
an engagement document, rather than, as Ian says,&nbsp;
as a sort of final document is the opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:29.120 --> 01:27:34.640
really hear back some of those sorts of really&nbsp;
helpful challenges, but also areas where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:35.280 --> 01:27:40.640
you know, no doubt, we need to do more on a whole&nbsp;
range of areas. And I do wonder whether the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.640 --> 01:27:47.120
that you’ve just pointed out, David, that where we&nbsp;
talk about person-centred care is in digital data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:47.120 --> 01:27:53.360
we might be doing ourselves a bit of a disservice&nbsp;
by putting it there. Because actually, what it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:53.360 --> 01:28:00.080
about is using digital and data, of course, as a&nbsp;
way of giving people a different kind of control&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:00.080 --> 01:28:05.920
over their interaction with healthcare rather,&nbsp;
perhaps than something we talked about earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:05.920 --> 01:28:10.960
rather than the more traditional models&nbsp;
we’ve got at the moment. But actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:10.960 --> 01:28:13.760
of course, it’s so much more than that.
And when we talk about this, when we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:13.760 --> 01:28:17.520
talked about, I’ve talked to colleagues about&nbsp;
this over the last few weeks and months, actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.520 --> 01:28:22.560
it is the person-centred care that people get&nbsp;
really excited about. That and population health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:22.560 --> 01:28:26.240
management, those are the two things that&nbsp;
when you start talking about it, people go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:26.240 --> 01:28:32.960
well, that’s exactly what we want to be doing.
And so what, what I think we need to do is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:32.960 --> 01:28:38.240
probably check ourselves, don’t we, that that’s&nbsp;
written into all of these different aspects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:38.240 --> 01:28:45.120
policy and practice that we’re now developing&nbsp;
over the course of 21/22, so that it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:45.120 --> 01:28:50.720
feel like it’s something that we’ve put in just&nbsp;
in one box. So for example, I’m aware we’ve said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:51.280 --> 01:28:55.200
you know, health watch need to be around the&nbsp;
table as part of the governance arrangements&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:55.200 --> 01:29:00.640
at place, and we’ve talked about some of the&nbsp;
governance arrangements, I think explicitly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:00.640 --> 01:29:06.400
with a view to the sort of patient voice&nbsp;
being very much an expected part of that.&nbsp;

01:29:06.400 --> 01:29:10.080
But let’s take that challenge away and&nbsp;
see whether that’s something we can write&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:10.080 --> 01:29:14.000
up and go further with, because it’s certainly at&nbsp;
the heart of what this whole endeavour is about.&nbsp;

01:29:14.000 --> 01:29:18.480
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: David, might I come in on that&nbsp;
specific point around personalised care?&nbsp;

01:29:18.480 --> 01:29:21.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Who is that? Is that Ian?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: That’s me, yeah.&nbsp;

01:29:21.600 --> 01:29:23.840
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ian, yes please.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Great, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:25.120 --> 01:29:29.280
and just picking up Amanda on your response,&nbsp;
and, David, your question, which I think is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:29.280 --> 01:29:34.160
an incredibly important question. I think we&nbsp;
probably haven’t done justice in the document&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:34.160 --> 01:29:40.960
to the fantastic work that we’re driving through&nbsp;
James Sanderson’s group on personalised care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:41.760 --> 01:29:49.200
writ large in the long term plan in the very first&nbsp;
chapter is the implementation of the NHS model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:49.200 --> 01:29:55.360
the comprehensive NHS model of personalised care.
We have been, it’s got a number of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:55.360 --> 01:30:00.240
core components, and we’ve been continuing to&nbsp;
drive that throughout the pandemic. There’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:00.240 --> 01:30:06.800
huge focus on shared decision making, on the&nbsp;
rollout of personalised care and support plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:08.000 --> 01:30:15.600
for many of our most vulnerable patients, and&nbsp;
patient empowerment, including focusing on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:15.600 --> 01:30:22.800
wider non-healthcare needs. So the huge expansion&nbsp;
of social prescribing and the connection through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.440 --> 01:30:30.720
to other community services, and personal health&nbsp;
budgets, and we had a goal set by the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:30.720 --> 01:30:37.680
in the mandate to try and achieve at least&nbsp;
between 50 and 100,000 personal health budgets&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:37.680 --> 01:30:46.400
by 2021. We exceeded that goal, actually, by&nbsp;
the end of the last year. And I think there’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:46.400 --> 01:30:51.120
there’s a lot more that we’re continuing to do in&nbsp;
exactly this space that goes well beyond digital.&nbsp;

01:30:53.600 --> 01:30:58.160
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Ian. I think&nbsp;
we’re sort of coming to the end of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:58.160 --> 01:31:01.840
discussion on this topic. Simon, I think&nbsp;
you’re going to have a final word on this.&nbsp;

01:31:06.560 --> 01:31:10.560
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thank you, David. Well, it was&nbsp;
just on the legislative point, really, underlining&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:10.560 --> 01:31:16.240
the discussion that we’ve been having, and the&nbsp;
evolutionary nature of what’s proposed here. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:16.240 --> 01:31:20.880
came across this rather good quote, which&nbsp;
I think characterises the approach we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:20.880 --> 01:31:25.360
taking. It’s a quote from the patron saint of&nbsp;
the National Health Service, Aneurin Bevan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:26.160 --> 01:31:31.280
And he said that no legislation however&nbsp;
wisely conceived and how efficiently&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:31.280 --> 01:31:36.240
embodied in an act of parliament can ever give&nbsp;
the public a great health service, unless the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:36.240 --> 01:31:42.720
people who administer it want to do it and are&nbsp;
enthusiastic in doing it. He went on to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:42.720 --> 01:31:46.960
that is the progress of legislation in this&nbsp;
country. It starts off by voluntary effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:46.960 --> 01:31:52.640
it starts off by empirical experiment, it starts&nbsp;
by improvisation. It establishes itself by merit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:52.640 --> 01:31:57.440
And ultimately, at some stage or other the&nbsp;
state steps in and makes what was started by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:57.440 --> 01:32:02.880
voluntary action and experiment a universal&nbsp;
service. And that is what we’re proposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:02.880 --> 01:32:06.560
with the integrated care systems.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:06.560 --> 01:32:11.840
Simon, you might like to send that quote to Andrew&nbsp;
Lansley and see what he makes of it in that case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:13.360 --> 01:32:19.440
Anyway, that’s a very good quote. And I think&nbsp;
that really encapsulates exactly what we’re on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:19.440 --> 01:32:23.840
about at the moment, the integrated care systems,&nbsp;
absolutely down to the to a tee. So that’s a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:23.840 --> 01:32:30.480
good place to end it on. Thank you very much&nbsp;
for that. Amanda, Bill, Ian, everyone who’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:30.480 --> 01:32:34.400
been involved in this. Thank you very much.&nbsp;
I think we’re in a very good place on this.&nbsp;

01:32:34.400 --> 01:32:39.886
So moving on to the next item, Prerana.
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Hello, David.&nbsp;

01:32:39.886 --> 01:32:42.000
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And I think is&nbsp;
Claire Murdoch here as well?&nbsp;

01:32:42.000 --> 01:32:46.880
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Hello, yes. Good afternoon.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Oh, hi, Claire. Prerana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:48.080 --> 01:32:49.520
over to you.
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:49.520 --> 01:32:54.320
you very much. Thanks for the time and&nbsp;
focus on staff health and wellbeing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:55.040 --> 01:32:59.920
I first want to start by acknowledging the&nbsp;
extraordinary work that NHS staff have done well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:59.920 --> 01:33:04.960
ever since Nye Bevan, and the quote that Simon&nbsp;
just shared, but actually, especially in the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:05.520 --> 01:33:10.800
seven, eight months, to respond to the pandemic,&nbsp;
not just NHS staff, but all healthcare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:10.800 --> 01:33:15.840
social care, and all essential workers. So&nbsp;
a huge thank you and an acknowledgement.&nbsp;

01:33:15.840 --> 01:33:20.080
And the conversation about health and wellbeing&nbsp;
support to staff is very much rooted in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:20.080 --> 01:33:26.000
challenge that staff are facing in providing care.&nbsp;
And I wanted to share some practical steps that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:26.000 --> 01:33:32.720
we’ve taken and then my colleague Claire is going&nbsp;
to talk about the treatment assessment aspects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:32.720 --> 01:33:38.640
the offer that we have, that we’re building.
So within three weeks of the pandemic and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:38.640 --> 01:33:44.080
emergency being declared, we worked very quickly&nbsp;
on a health and wellbeing offer at the national&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:44.080 --> 01:33:49.120
level to augment what were really, really&nbsp;
supportive offers at local levels as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:49.120 --> 01:33:54.480
by local employers, by local businesses,&nbsp;
by local voluntary sector organisations.&nbsp;

01:33:54.480 --> 01:34:00.560
So on the 7th of April, we launched a national&nbsp;
offer which was available to all 1.3 million NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:00.560 --> 01:34:06.960
staff. And some aspects of that offer were also&nbsp;
available to social care staff. The parts where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:06.960 --> 01:34:12.960
they weren’t available to social care was because&nbsp;
of funding constraints and you know, the nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:13.680 --> 01:34:18.880
the service that we were providing. So first,&nbsp;
also a shout out to our partners. This health and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:18.880 --> 01:34:25.440
wellbeing offer, you know, was made possible in&nbsp;
many cases because the entire country got behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:25.440 --> 01:34:31.680
NHS staff. So this was at local level, offers&nbsp;
of transport, offers of food, free, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:31.680 --> 01:34:39.120
food and drink, supermarkets who had&nbsp;
packages made up for NHS staff that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:39.120 --> 01:34:45.280
placed vans in car parks of providers so that&nbsp;
workers coming off shifts would have access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:45.280 --> 01:34:51.520
groceries. So just a whole host of local offers.
And at the same time in the spirit of do once&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:51.520 --> 01:34:57.040
a small number of things that should be done&nbsp;
nationally, we convened an expert reference group&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:57.040 --> 01:35:01.600
made up of people who have been in emergencies&nbsp;
before, who have worked with the WHO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:01.600 --> 01:35:05.840
who have been in complex emergencies&nbsp;
and incidents in the UK, who advised us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:05.840 --> 01:35:11.440
the kind of support that staff would need.
This expert reference group has helped us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:11.440 --> 01:35:15.040
have an evidence-based approach, we&nbsp;
continue to work with them actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:15.040 --> 01:35:21.360
and as the nature of the pandemic has changed,&nbsp;
and our responses have changed, we have also built&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:21.360 --> 01:35:28.800
on that offer with their advice and support.
So it is a model which is based on the three&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:28.800 --> 01:35:34.400
stages of prepare, active and recover. Those&nbsp;
are the three stages of an emergency response.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:34.400 --> 01:35:37.600
And actually a lot, you know, there&nbsp;
wasn’t a lot of time for prepare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:38.640 --> 01:35:44.240
And we are not yet in recover. So all of&nbsp;
our support is focused on the active phase.&nbsp;

01:35:45.120 --> 01:35:50.560
The model is based on an end to end pathway.&nbsp;
They’ve advised us very clearly not to over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:50.560 --> 01:35:56.240
medicalise the kind of support we should&nbsp;
give staff. It’s focused on prevention and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:56.240 --> 01:36:00.880
self-help as the majority of the support, and&nbsp;
I’ll just detail a few things that we’re doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:01.920 --> 01:36:07.840
on that end. And then a small, focused&nbsp;
amount of support on those who need more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:07.840 --> 01:36:12.080
medical intervention, and Claire will speak&nbsp;
about the mental health aspects of that.&nbsp;

01:36:12.640 --> 01:36:16.960
So the second aspect is that people&nbsp;
get their support from teams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:16.960 --> 01:36:25.200
resilience resides in teams, and that’s trademark&nbsp;
Claire Murdoch, I must give her credit for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:25.200 --> 01:36:31.520
concept. And what we have then done is to make&nbsp;
sure that we have tools and resources available&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:31.520 --> 01:36:36.720
for teams to be able to support each other.
From feedback, we know that two things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:36.720 --> 01:36:41.520
we have introduced for teams have had the&nbsp;
maximum impact in terms of resilience. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:41.520 --> 01:36:46.240
is what we call the 10-minute pause. I mean,&nbsp;
not we but it’s called the 10 minute pause,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:46.240 --> 01:36:53.680
where we are encouraging teams, especially after&nbsp;
stressful incidents during a busy shift to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:53.680 --> 01:36:58.880
that 10 minute pause, where someone can step out&nbsp;
and you know, centre themselves and come back in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:00.400 --> 01:37:03.680
It helped people to have permission&nbsp;
to do that, and we’ve really been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:03.680 --> 01:37:07.040
pushing this 10 minute pause.
The second is the leaving well&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:07.040 --> 01:37:13.360
checklist where there is, you know, a way that&nbsp;
you can leave a shift where you can go home&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:13.360 --> 01:37:17.600
and recover and then be able to come back&nbsp;
and provide compassionate care the next day.&nbsp;

01:37:18.560 --> 01:37:22.800
These are the two key&nbsp;
interventions at a team level.&nbsp;

01:37:22.800 --> 01:37:30.160
On the self help level, we have 153,000&nbsp;
people who are using the mental health apps&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:30.160 --> 01:37:36.480
that are available free. And again, shout out to&nbsp;
partners who have made this possible. What we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:36.480 --> 01:37:41.200
also done is inbuilt feedback throughout this&nbsp;
whole process so actually when people use an app,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:41.200 --> 01:37:46.880
they provide feedback and we of course in&nbsp;
an anonymised way get that feedback and can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:46.880 --> 01:37:52.240
in real time make changes and augment our offer.&nbsp;
So for instance, mental health apps are showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:52.240 --> 01:37:57.040
that it’s actually helping people to sleep better&nbsp;
than before, actually even before Covid, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:57.040 --> 01:38:02.000
we have to remember that, you know, health and&nbsp;
wellbeing was an area of focus, but not as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:02.720 --> 01:38:09.040
an area of challenge before Covid as well.&nbsp;
And one of the you know, one of the big shifts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:09.040 --> 01:38:14.720
in our culture in the last few months in&nbsp;
the NHS has been the enhanced focus on staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:14.720 --> 01:38:19.680
health and wellbeing, which is absolutely right.
So we are having a constant dialogue with staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:19.680 --> 01:38:23.200
a few enhancements to the health and&nbsp;
wellbeing offer as a result of their feedback.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:23.760 --> 01:38:30.880
One was that our colleagues from the&nbsp;
Philippines who had tragically a high loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:30.880 --> 01:38:38.480
of their colleagues, relatively speaking, asked&nbsp;
us for a bespoke bereavement service in Tagalog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:38.480 --> 01:38:45.600
and we made that available along with Hospice UK.
We had feedback that people were suffering from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:45.600 --> 01:38:50.240
secondary stressors. So what they bring into&nbsp;
work is of course their whole self and as we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:50.240 --> 01:38:57.120
impact on the economy, we have people who are&nbsp;
concerned about family members’, you know, jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:57.120 --> 01:39:03.120
and financial situation, so we have partnered&nbsp;
with Money Advice Programme and we provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:03.120 --> 01:39:07.440
financial advice that people are taking&nbsp;
up, this started on the 1st of October.&nbsp;

01:39:08.000 --> 01:39:13.120
We are also making sure that we’re normalising,&nbsp;
in fact supporting, help-seeking behaviour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:13.120 --> 01:39:18.240
because research shows that healthcare staff&nbsp;
and social care staff are amongst the last to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:18.240 --> 01:39:23.200
seek help for themselves. So although people are&nbsp;
doing extraordinary work, our clinical reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:23.200 --> 01:39:28.080
group has advised that we shouldn’t label it&nbsp;
as heroic and heroes because actually that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:29.360 --> 01:39:35.120
keeps people from seeking help. So we you&nbsp;
know, we’re being quite, well hopefully being&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:35.120 --> 01:39:39.680
quite thoughtful about how we’re engaging and&nbsp;
communicating not only that the offer is there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:39.680 --> 01:39:48.240
but actually how to access and why to access.
We have also made like the Filipino support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:48.240 --> 01:39:54.240
other support available to BAME staff. We&nbsp;
have been convening a national BAME staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:54.240 --> 01:39:59.760
network conversation that I have convened since&nbsp;
April, where we asked directly what staff need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:59.760 --> 01:40:04.640
especially as we see the disproportionate impact&nbsp;
of Covid. So actually the risk assessments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:04.640 --> 01:40:10.080
which was a safety, staff safety and health and&nbsp;
wellbeing intervention came from that group.&nbsp;

01:40:10.720 --> 01:40:16.720
We are also working with faith groups on health&nbsp;
and wellbeing and BAME staff especially had said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:16.720 --> 01:40:21.040
that they get a lot of the, you know, pre&nbsp;
Covid used to get a lot of their support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:21.040 --> 01:40:26.800
from places of worship and, and faith groups.&nbsp;
And with that aspect not available, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:26.800 --> 01:40:31.840
worked with faith groups to connect directly to&nbsp;
our staff and, you know, in Covid-friendly ways.&nbsp;

01:40:33.360 --> 01:40:38.320
I wanted to draw the board’s attention in the&nbsp;
paper to some real quotes which are on page&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:38.320 --> 01:40:43.200
four and they are from real people and gives&nbsp;
you a flavour of how people have experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:43.200 --> 01:40:49.120
the health and wellbeing offer.
I think, before I hand over to Claire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:49.120 --> 01:40:53.920
I would like to say, as Amanda said right at the&nbsp;
beginning, this shift in health and wellbeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:53.920 --> 01:41:00.640
support must remain one of the enduring positive&nbsp;
changes out of Covid. With this, we really can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:00.640 --> 01:41:07.040
go back on this, because, you know, we know that&nbsp;
some of the challenges were there before Covid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:07.040 --> 01:41:12.400
as well, and we were saying, in our team, one of&nbsp;
our learnings is that what I think in many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:12.960 --> 01:41:18.160
parts of the service, what would have taken&nbsp;
probably three years to achieve was possible in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:18.160 --> 01:41:22.720
a very short in a matter of weeks. And that,&nbsp;
you know, we must hang on to and build on.&nbsp;

01:41:24.640 --> 01:41:29.200
Our experts say that the biggest thing that&nbsp;
will help people recover is the sense of hope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:29.200 --> 01:41:32.160
So in leadership messages, in engagements with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:32.800 --> 01:41:38.480
managers with their teams, we must remind people&nbsp;
that the pandemic will end one day and soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:38.480 --> 01:41:44.160
and that, you know, the positive changes that&nbsp;
we’ve seen, whether it’s flexible working,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:44.160 --> 01:41:49.280
increased use of technology etc, and health&nbsp;
and wellbeing are here to stay. So that’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:49.280 --> 01:41:54.640
advice from the expert group for leaders.
And then there’s been increased investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:55.520 --> 01:42:02.640
of 15 million for all regions to have health and&nbsp;
wellbeing hubs, mental health and wellbeing hubs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:02.640 --> 01:42:08.000
And then enhanced occupational health and&nbsp;
wellbeing services in 14 systems being piloted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:08.000 --> 01:42:15.600
that I wanted to hand over to Claire. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Yes, thank you very much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:15.600 --> 01:42:20.000
that. Just by way of introduction, I’m&nbsp;
the national director for mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:20.800 --> 01:42:27.440
in England. I’m also a trust chief executive,&nbsp;
we have 7,000 staff colleagues in my trust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:27.440 --> 01:42:34.160
and I’ve worked in the NHS for 37 years. And I&nbsp;
have to say, I’ve never been more proud to be part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.160 --> 01:42:42.880
of the NHS. I’ve never seen more determination&nbsp;
to do the right thing by patients and each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:43.680 --> 01:42:47.680
And I would just like to say,&nbsp;
I think we have to resist as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:48.320 --> 01:42:54.480
think about how we support our staff through this,&nbsp;
we have to resist thinking that our staff are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:55.280 --> 01:43:02.240
on their knees or in despair. And I think that&nbsp;
when I visit different places across the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:02.240 --> 01:43:07.600
or across my own organisation, I hear&nbsp;
the most extraordinary stories of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:07.600 --> 01:43:14.720
well-trained, values-driven hard-working&nbsp;
colleagues are actually, in many cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:14.720 --> 01:43:18.960
quite upbeat about what they’re doing&nbsp;
and pleased to share what they’re doing.&nbsp;

01:43:18.960 --> 01:43:26.240
I think Prerana is absolutely right that there’s&nbsp;
a determination to leave a legacy out of what has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:26.240 --> 01:43:31.840
undoubtedly also been a really tough time, and&nbsp;
for some teams, and in some places, it’s been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:31.840 --> 01:43:37.520
extraordinarily tough. But there’s a determination&nbsp;
to leave legacy. And I think some of what we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:37.520 --> 01:43:44.800
learned has been that staff have felt in many&nbsp;
parts of the country heard, given more licence&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:44.800 --> 01:43:51.360
to act, more freedoms to move quickly, to do the&nbsp;
right thing. They have appreciated the additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:51.360 --> 01:43:59.040
money currently, for example, I know there was a&nbsp;
big cheer in many parts of my sector but beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:59.040 --> 01:44:04.960
about the additional money that we will get&nbsp;
to help us through winter and into next year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:04.960 --> 01:44:08.880
because that drives very practical&nbsp;
supports, whether that’s more staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:08.880 --> 01:44:14.720
better environments, and so on and so forth.
So I think that there’s quite a lot to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:14.720 --> 01:44:19.040
and it’s to link to Prerana’s&nbsp;
point really, hopeful about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:19.040 --> 01:44:25.920
Never complacent but absolutely always hopeful.
I think some of the other things that we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:25.920 --> 01:44:31.120
be hopeful about is I understand that more than&nbsp;
10,000 people have already volunteered to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:31.120 --> 01:44:36.480
to the NHS and help with the vaccination effort.&nbsp;
I understand we’ve got more people coming forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:36.480 --> 01:44:42.080
for example, for nurse recruitment than ever&nbsp;
before and thank you very much to Prerana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:43.280 --> 01:44:47.440
our Chief Nurse and others, Health&nbsp;
Education England for driving that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:47.440 --> 01:44:53.200
There are, because having enough staff is&nbsp;
a critical part actually of staff morale&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:53.200 --> 01:44:57.760
and staff welfare. When you’re working&nbsp;
in teams where you have enough staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:57.760 --> 01:45:01.280
you know you can take your rest days.&nbsp;
You know you can take your holiday.&nbsp;

01:45:01.280 --> 01:45:06.240
And we’ve certainly been talking about things&nbsp;
like making sure people take their leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:06.240 --> 01:45:11.200
even now, even though they can’t jet off&nbsp;
to the Caribbean or Spain at the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:11.920 --> 01:45:17.840
having their breaks, having downtime away from&nbsp;
the keyboard, away from frontline clinical work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:17.840 --> 01:45:23.280
is critical through this period. So we&nbsp;
need to do those practical things as well.&nbsp;

01:45:23.280 --> 01:45:28.960
But thank you very much to Simon, Amanda, and&nbsp;
everyone who has, and Julian, who’s got us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:28.960 --> 01:45:35.040
that additional very practical investment.
Prerana is quite right. Resilience resides&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:35.040 --> 01:45:38.960
within teams. And one of the things I’ve&nbsp;
heard from many teams across this last&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:38.960 --> 01:45:44.080
period that we have to build on, is they&nbsp;
felt much more free to act to do the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:44.080 --> 01:45:49.280
thing. And they’ve never had so many conversations&nbsp;
with their executive boards, chief executives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:49.280 --> 01:45:54.080
medical directors, and nurse directors.&nbsp;
Why? Because we’ve been digitally enabled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:54.080 --> 01:46:00.000
to reach into the corners of our organisations.
Anyway, so I think there is much to be hopeful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:00.000 --> 01:46:06.400
about, but of course, never complacent that this&nbsp;
is going to be a tough winter. But our staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:06.400 --> 01:46:11.280
colleagues never cease to amaze me. There’s also&nbsp;
quite a lot of fun out there on occasion as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:11.280 --> 01:46:15.440
I think. And I think we have to value that&nbsp;
too, and create time for that as well.&nbsp;

01:46:16.080 --> 01:46:22.080
But to come to more, or a very serious point,&nbsp;
we know that for a small number of our staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:22.080 --> 01:46:27.040
in addition to everything that we’re doing to&nbsp;
support them, a small number will experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:27.920 --> 01:46:33.360
anxiety, depression, or other mental health&nbsp;
problems to a point where they are suffering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:34.240 --> 01:46:42.960
What we’re wanting to do with this additional&nbsp;
£15 million that we have is to establish these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:42.960 --> 01:46:51.280
hubs across staff wellbeing hubs across all 44&nbsp;
of our STP, ICS or local systems. Many of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:51.280 --> 01:46:56.640
are already in play, and were during wave one,&nbsp;
so we’re building on learning from that. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:56.640 --> 01:47:02.320
the key aim is to make sure that we can rapidly&nbsp;
assess and treat those people who are referred&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:02.320 --> 01:47:08.400
either by themselves, or through occupational&nbsp;
health or some other route. That’s one element.&nbsp;

01:47:08.400 --> 01:47:12.640
A second element is for those staff&nbsp;
who have particularly complex needs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:12.640 --> 01:47:18.720
we are building a national referral&nbsp;
route to get them more bespoke treatment.&nbsp;

01:47:18.720 --> 01:47:23.440
And a third element which the&nbsp;
Chief Nurse is leading is training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:23.440 --> 01:47:30.160
psychological wellbeing training for critical care&nbsp;
staff. Because actually, you can build that into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:30.720 --> 01:47:34.880
you absolutely can, and the evidence&nbsp;
shows that and there’s learning also from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:34.880 --> 01:47:40.080
colleagues in the military and other&nbsp;
sectors, that you can train yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:40.080 --> 01:47:45.920
to build that resilience into how teams&nbsp;
work, how you spot stress in each other.&nbsp;

01:47:45.920 --> 01:47:50.800
So there are three particular&nbsp;
components to the assessment treatment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:50.800 --> 01:47:56.400
specialist treatment at a national level, and then&nbsp;
also the psychological wellbeing and training.&nbsp;

01:47:56.400 --> 01:48:02.240
So of course, I’m delighted that we’re building&nbsp;
that particular bespoke mental health offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:02.240 --> 01:48:07.600
into the overall wellbeing offer. But I would&nbsp;
just like to end this little bit if I might&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:07.600 --> 01:48:14.400
where I started, which is to say, I see a huge&nbsp;
amount of resilience, professionalism, hard work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:15.680 --> 01:48:21.280
right across the NHS, even if we sit here now.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Claire and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:21.280 --> 01:48:24.400
Prerana. Wol.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:24.400 --> 01:48:29.520
Thank you, David. It’s just a comment very&nbsp;
quickly. Prerana, I think that the, one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:29.520 --> 01:48:34.400
the issues is that whilst we’re sort of still&nbsp;
fighting and hopefully finishing this battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:34.400 --> 01:48:38.480
war campaign, whatever we call it on Covid,&nbsp;
all these things stay on the agenda, and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:38.480 --> 01:48:42.640
very important and people get focused on it.
But let’s be honest, the further we move away&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:42.640 --> 01:48:48.320
from what was the crisis, the less and less focus&nbsp;
gets put on it. I would really encourage you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:48.320 --> 01:48:54.080
find a way of ensuring this stays on the&nbsp;
agenda, and the board is fully cited on it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:55.440 --> 01:49:04.480
So whether it’s final, final, you know,&nbsp;
reports or focus, that’s the first thing.&nbsp;

01:49:04.480 --> 01:49:10.080
The second thing is, I really think that given&nbsp;
what the NHS has done in terms of this pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:10.080 --> 01:49:16.880
we should be the exemplar for what good looks&nbsp;
like across the UK in, definitely the UK&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:16.880 --> 01:49:20.640
in its activity, given the number of staff&nbsp;
you’ve got and what they’ve gone through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:20.640 --> 01:49:25.440
So rather than following, we should be leading.&nbsp;
So those are my two challenges for you.&nbsp;

01:49:27.120 --> 01:49:34.480
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Wol. Any other&nbsp;
observations or questions for Prerana or Claire?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:36.800 --> 01:49:39.920
Claire, could you just give&nbsp;
us a sort of insight on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:40.640 --> 01:49:44.800
what it’s like in your trust at the&nbsp;
moment and you know, how you’re coping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:44.800 --> 01:49:49.680
whether you’re seeing a lot more demand in your&nbsp;
trust, and not just from a staff perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:49.680 --> 01:49:55.840
but from an overall perspective?
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Yes. We’re busy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:56.640 --> 01:50:05.600
of course, and we’re happily seeing a recovery&nbsp;
of restoration of the trajectories referrals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:05.600 --> 01:50:10.000
you know, we’re seeing more patients, some of&nbsp;
the stuff that tailed off during, some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:10.000 --> 01:50:16.240
referrals that tailed off during wave one, are&nbsp;
picking up apace, so we know we’re really busy.&nbsp;

01:50:16.240 --> 01:50:22.320
Certainly the urgent emergency care&nbsp;
pathways, both in my own trust and elsewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:22.320 --> 01:50:28.240
in mental health are very busy, which is why we’re&nbsp;
so delighted to have had the additional 50 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:28.240 --> 01:50:34.560
nationally, not all for my trust, that we’re&nbsp;
rolling out now to help support that pathway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:35.760 --> 01:50:44.400
I think there’s a big determination and largely&nbsp;
quite an optimism overall, that we can get through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:44.400 --> 01:50:51.360
this. I think the vaccine on the horizon helps.&nbsp;
I think we’ve got opportunity to further build on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:51.360 --> 01:50:57.920
the innovation that we discovered during wave one.
So some of the things that we just did during&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:57.920 --> 01:51:05.040
wave one, like we just did break down more of the&nbsp;
barriers between physical and mental health care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:05.040 --> 01:51:09.920
So if the mental health unit needed the district&nbsp;
nurses or community nurses to go in and further&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:09.920 --> 01:51:15.200
advise on physical health care, we just did it.&nbsp;
We didn’t look at the contract and who could do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:15.200 --> 01:51:24.240
what if we needed to support care home staff, we&nbsp;
just went in and did it, and across primary care.&nbsp;

01:51:24.240 --> 01:51:31.040
So people are tired, we’re taking really seriously&nbsp;
as I think every chief executive in the trust is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:31.040 --> 01:51:35.760
are people getting breaks, what are their&nbsp;
rotas like, are we making sure they get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:35.760 --> 01:51:41.920
downtime? We’re focusing on recruitment.&nbsp;
So really we’ve got lots of intent.&nbsp;

01:51:42.960 --> 01:51:47.200
One of the bits of feedback in my own trust,&nbsp;
which resonates right across the country that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:47.200 --> 01:51:55.920
was very positive from wave one was people enjoyed&nbsp;
having more regular weekly, or bi-weekly briefings&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:55.920 --> 01:52:02.880
over digital applications, they felt it collapsed&nbsp;
the tiers of the organisation, and that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:02.880 --> 01:52:08.160
able to input their ideas, ask their questions&nbsp;
to top teams, it really helped the top teams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:08.160 --> 01:52:11.360
because you had an absolute finger&nbsp;
on the pulse with what it’s like.&nbsp;

01:52:12.320 --> 01:52:17.680
I was really pleased, Prerana mentioned overseas&nbsp;
staff, of course we’re keen to see staff who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:17.680 --> 01:52:24.320
are used to going overseas to visit families&nbsp;
who haven’t been able to for a long time now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:24.880 --> 01:52:29.120
we’re already thinking about&nbsp;
holidays as we start to recover&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:29.120 --> 01:52:34.400
and how we smooth workforce across that period,&nbsp;
and we’re doing that with staff and staff side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:34.400 --> 01:52:41.360
I think these things are happening across the&nbsp;
country in every trust and across health care.&nbsp;

01:52:43.520 --> 01:52:51.040
But we’re working really hard. No doubt about&nbsp;
it, but there’s a strong sense of purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:51.600 --> 01:52:57.840
And the additional workforce, the&nbsp;
additional funding really, really help.&nbsp;

01:52:57.840 --> 01:53:03.280
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Claire. Steve&nbsp;
Powis and then Prerana, I’ll go to Prerana. Yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:05.040 --> 01:53:05.840
Steve.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:06.640 --> 01:53:11.120
it was really just say how welcome this&nbsp;
is. I think Ruth referred to, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:11.120 --> 01:53:15.280
the huge amount of work that staff are doing&nbsp;
earlier on in the board meeting, and I certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:15.280 --> 01:53:21.120
hear the same from my colleagues in doctor&nbsp;
leadership roles and medical leadership roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:21.760 --> 01:53:26.240
And it’s been an incredibly challenging year,&nbsp;
it’s going to be an incredibly challenging winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:26.960 --> 01:53:32.560
but as you said, Prerana, this is not just for&nbsp;
this winter, this needs to be for years ahead and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:32.560 --> 01:53:36.160
years to come. So I just want to say thank you for&nbsp;
all the hard work you and the team are putting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:37.120 --> 01:53:42.320
and how welcome this is across the system.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Steve. So, Prerana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:42.320 --> 01:53:46.000
last word from you before we wrap up.
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Thank you. And thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:46.000 --> 01:53:49.120
for the comments. I was actually, I’ve seen&nbsp;
Wol had to drop out, but I was actually going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:49.120 --> 01:53:55.920
to tell him that we just the team, my team who&nbsp;
worked on the health and wellbeing has just won&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:56.480 --> 01:54:00.240
an award in the last five minutes, it’s&nbsp;
been announced, for the health and wellbeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:00.240 --> 01:54:06.480
initiative of the year. And this is a national&nbsp;
level award by Personnel Today, which is which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:06.480 --> 01:54:12.080
looks at people practices. So I will send an email&nbsp;
to all that actually, we are leading in some ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:12.080 --> 01:54:18.080
and being recognised for that. But I think it’s&nbsp;
important that the board as you’ve said, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:18.080 --> 01:54:24.720
holds us accountable for continuing with this&nbsp;
effort. We want our staff to feel treasured, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:24.720 --> 01:54:31.600
want the work they do to continue to be treasured.&nbsp;
And this is very much a part of our recognition&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:31.600 --> 01:54:35.440
of the work that they do. Thanks very much.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Prerana, thank you for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:35.440 --> 01:54:40.400
and congratulations on the award.&nbsp;
And as Wol said, we should have this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:40.400 --> 01:54:46.080
we should have this as a regular part of the board&nbsp;
agenda going forward, whether it’s quarterly or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:46.080 --> 01:54:50.240
however we do it, we should definitely,&nbsp;
so we’re all kept up to date with this.&nbsp;

01:54:50.240 --> 01:54:52.880
So thank you. Thank you both very&nbsp;
much indeed. That’s terrific.&nbsp;

01:54:53.920 --> 01:54:58.560
I think the last item is just a formality which is&nbsp;
just the use of the NHS seal which I think we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:58.560 --> 01:55:04.080
note, Sophia, don’t we, for the purpose of this&nbsp;
meeting. In which case, I think, unless there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:04.080 --> 01:55:09.360
any other business, I think we’ll bring it to&nbsp;
a close. Thank you all very much. Thank you.

